problem.md:1:\documentclass[11pt]{article}
problem.md:2:
problem.md:3:\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
problem.md:4:\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}
problem.md:5:\usepackage{enumitem}
problem.md:6:
problem.md:7:\newtheorem{assumption}{Assumption}
problem.md:8:\newtheorem{conjecture}{Conjecture}
problem.md:9:\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
problem.md:10:
problem.md:11:\newcommand{\ind}[1]{\mathbf{1}\{#1\}}
problem.md:12:
problem.md:13:\title{Delayed Market Making}
problem.md:14:\author{}
problem.md:15:\date{\today}
problem.md:16:
problem.md:17:\begin{document}
problem.md:18:\maketitle
problem.md:19:
problem.md:20:At step $t$:
problem.md:21:\begin{enumerate}
problem.md:22:    \item Market reveals $P_t \in [0,1]$.
problem.md:23:    \item Learner picks $a_t \in [0,1]$.
problem.md:24:    \item For all $t' < t$, if $a_{t'} < P_t$, then $a_{t'}$ gets traded and the learner gets reward $a_{t'}$.
problem.md:25:\end{enumerate}
problem.md:26:
problem.md:27:Let
problem.md:28:\[
problem.md:29:\tau_t(a) := \min \{s > 0 : P_{t+s} > a\}.
problem.md:30:\]
problem.md:31:
problem.md:32:Then the reward from posting quote $a$ at time $t$ is
problem.md:33:\[
problem.md:34:r_t(a) = a \cdot \ind{\tau_t(a) \le T-t}.
problem.md:35:\]
problem.md:36:
problem.md:37:The regret is
problem.md:38:\[
problem.md:39:R(T)
problem.md:40:=
problem.md:41:\max_{a \in [0,1]} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\right]
problem.md:42:-
problem.md:43:\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a_t)\right].
problem.md:44:\]
problem.md:45:
problem.md:46:\begin{assumption}\label{ass:tail-delays}
problem.md:47:Let
problem.md:48:\[
problem.md:49:\beta(h)
problem.md:50::=
problem.md:51:\sup_t \sup_a
problem.md:52:\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{P_1,\dots,P_t}
problem.md:53:\Pr\bigl[h < \tau_t(a) < \infty \mid P_1,\dots,P_t\bigr].
problem.md:54:\]
problem.md:55:Assume
problem.md:56:\[
problem.md:57:\lim_{h \to \infty} \beta(h)
problem.md:58:=
problem.md:59:0.
problem.md:60:\]
problem.md:61:\end{assumption}
problem.md:62:
problem.md:63:\begin{conjecture}
problem.md:64:Sublinear regret is possible if and only if Assumption~\ref{ass:tail-delays}
problem.md:65:holds.
problem.md:66:\end{conjecture}
problem.md:67:
problem.md:68:\begin{proof}
problem.md:69:\emph{If direction.}
problem.md:70:
problem.md:71:\begin{itemize}
problem.md:72:    \item Assume $K$ arms. Later, we will control the error due to discretization. (Lemma~\ref{lem:discretization-comparison})
problem.md:73:
problem.md:74:    \item Pick some $H(T)$ that is sublinear. We will optimize the exact form of $H(T)$ for optimal regret.
problem.md:75:
problem.md:76:    \item Run an algorithm for expert advice with $K$ arms and delay $H(T)$. If a quote has not traded after $H(T)$ steps, assume it will never trade.
problem.md:77:
problem.md:78:    \item Consider the learning problem you get if the delay actually has an upper
problem.md:79:    bound of $H(T)$, i.e., define
problem.md:80:    \[
problem.md:81:        r^H_t(a) = a\cdot \ind{\tau_t(a) \leq \min\{H(T), T-t\}}
problem.md:82:    \]
problem.md:83:    and the corresponding regret
problem.md:84:    \[
problem.md:85:        R^H(T)
problem.md:86:        =
problem.md:87:        \max_{a \in [0,1]} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:88:        -
problem.md:89:        \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a_t)\right].
problem.md:90:    \]
problem.md:91:
problem.md:92:    \item From standard expert advice with delay algorithms, we get $R^H(T) = O(\sqrt{TH(T)\log{K}})$
problem.md:93:
problem.md:94:    \item (Lemma~\ref{lem:truncation-comparison}) For each fixed $a$, we have that
problem.md:95:    \[
problem.md:96:        \mathbb{E}\left[r_t(a) - r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:97:        \leq
problem.md:98:        \Pr\bigl[H(T) < \tau_t(a) < \infty\bigr]
problem.md:99:        \leq
problem.md:100:        \beta(H(T)).
problem.md:101:    \]
problem.md:102:    Since $r_t(a_t) \geq r^H_t(a_t)$, it follows that
problem.md:103:    $R(T) \leq R^H(T) + T\beta(H(T))$, and therefore
problem.md:104:
problem.md:105:    \[
problem.md:106:    R(T) \leq O(\sqrt{TH(T)\log{K}}) + T\beta(H(T))
problem.md:107:    \]
problem.md:108:
problem.md:109:    \item Thus under Assumption~\ref{ass:tail-delays}, we get sublinear regret.
problem.md:110:    Furthermore, if we know the exact form of $\beta(h)$, then we can choose
problem.md:111:    $H(T)$ appropriately to balance the two terms.
problem.md:112:\end{itemize}
problem.md:113:
problem.md:114:Below are some details.
problem.md:115:
problem.md:116:\begin{lemma}[Truncation comparison]\label{lem:truncation-comparison}
problem.md:117:Let \(H = H(T)\), and let
problem.md:118:\[
problem.md:119:    r^H_t(a)
problem.md:120:    =
problem.md:121:    a\cdot \ind{\tau_t(a) \leq \min\{H,T-t\}}.
problem.md:122:\]
problem.md:123:Fix any learner and let \(R(T)\) and \(R^H(T)\) be its expected regrets under the rewards
problem.md:124:\(r_t\) and \(r^H_t\), respectively.
problem.md:125:Then
problem.md:126:\[
problem.md:127:    R(T) \leq R^H(T) + T\beta(H).
problem.md:128:\]
problem.md:129:\end{lemma}
problem.md:130:
problem.md:131:\begin{proof}
problem.md:132:Fix a quote \(a \in [0,1]\). The true reward \(r_t(a)\) and the truncated
problem.md:133:reward \(r^H_t(a)\) differ only when the quote trades before the end of the
problem.md:134:game, but more than \(H\) steps after it was posted. Thus
problem.md:135:\[
problem.md:136:    0
problem.md:137:    \leq
problem.md:138:    r_t(a) - r^H_t(a)
problem.md:139:    \leq
problem.md:140:    \ind{H < \tau_t(a) < \infty}.
problem.md:141:\]
problem.md:142:Taking conditional expectations given the realized history \(P_1,\dots,P_t\),
problem.md:143:we get
problem.md:144:\[
problem.md:145:    \mathbb{E}\left[
problem.md:146:        r_t(a) - r^H_t(a)
problem.md:147:        \mid P_1,\dots,P_t
problem.md:148:    \right]
problem.md:149:    \leq
problem.md:150:    \Pr\bigl[H < \tau_t(a) < \infty \mid P_1,\dots,P_t\bigr]
problem.md:151:    \leq
problem.md:152:    \beta(H),
problem.md:153:\]
problem.md:154:where the last inequality is exactly the definition of \(\beta(H)\). Taking
problem.md:155:expectations and summing over \(t\), this gives
problem.md:156:\[
problem.md:157:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\right]
problem.md:158:    \leq
problem.md:159:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:160:    +
problem.md:161:    T\beta(H).
problem.md:162:\]
problem.md:163:Since this holds for every fixed quote \(a\),
problem.md:164:\[
problem.md:165:    \max_{a \in [0,1]}
problem.md:166:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\right]
problem.md:167:    \leq
problem.md:168:    \max_{a \in [0,1]}
problem.md:169:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:170:    +
problem.md:171:    T\beta(H).
problem.md:172:\]
problem.md:173:
problem.md:174:On the other hand, along every realized path we have
problem.md:175:\(r^H_t(a_t) \leq r_t(a_t)\) for every \(t\). Therefore
problem.md:176:\[
problem.md:177:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a_t)\right]
problem.md:178:    \geq
problem.md:179:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a_t)\right].
problem.md:180:\]
problem.md:181:Combining, we get, 
problem.md:182:\[
problem.md:183:\begin{aligned}
problem.md:184:    R(T)
problem.md:185:    &=
problem.md:186:    \max_{a \in [0,1]}
problem.md:187:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\right]
problem.md:188:    -
problem.md:189:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a_t)\right] \\
problem.md:190:    &\leq
problem.md:191:    \max_{a \in [0,1]}
problem.md:192:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:193:    -
problem.md:194:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a_t)\right]
problem.md:195:    +
problem.md:196:    T\beta(H) \\
problem.md:197:    &=
problem.md:198:    R^H(T) + T\beta(H).
problem.md:199:\end{aligned}
problem.md:200:\]
problem.md:201:\end{proof}
problem.md:202:
problem.md:203:\begin{lemma}[Discretization comparison]\label{lem:discretization-comparison}
problem.md:204:Let \(K \geq 2\), and let
problem.md:205:\[
problem.md:206:    \mathcal{G}_K
problem.md:207:    =
problem.md:208:    \left\{0, \frac{1}{K}, \dots, \frac{K-1}{K}\right\}.
problem.md:209:\]
problem.md:210:Consider a grid-restricted problem where \(a_t \in
problem.md:211:\mathcal{G}_K\) for every \(t\). Define regret of a learner as
problem.md:212:\[
problem.md:213:    R^H_K(T)
problem.md:214:    =
problem.md:215:    \max_{b \in \mathcal{G}_K}
problem.md:216:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(b)\right]
problem.md:217:    -
problem.md:218:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a_t)\right].
problem.md:219:\]
problem.md:220:Let \(R^H(T)\) be the regret of the same learner in the original
problem.md:221:problem, where the comparator may choose any fixed action in \([0,1]\):
problem.md:222:\[
problem.md:223:    R^H(T)
problem.md:224:    =
problem.md:225:    \max_{a \in [0,1]}
problem.md:226:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:227:    -
problem.md:228:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a_t)\right].
problem.md:229:\]
problem.md:230:Then,
problem.md:231:\[
problem.md:232:    R^H(T) \leq R^H_K(T) + \frac{T}{K}.
problem.md:233:\]
problem.md:234:\end{lemma}
problem.md:235:
problem.md:236:\begin{proof}
problem.md:237:Let \(L_t = \min\{H,T-t\}\). For every quote \(a \in [0,1]\), choose a grid
problem.md:238:point \(b(a) \in \mathcal{G}_K\) such that
problem.md:239:\[
problem.md:240:    b(a) \leq a
problem.md:241:    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
problem.md:242:    a - b(a) \leq \frac{1}{K}.
problem.md:243:\]
problem.md:244:This is possible by rounding \(a\) down to the nearest point of
problem.md:245:\(\mathcal{G}_K\).
problem.md:246:
problem.md:247:Fix \(a\) and write \(b = b(a)\). Since \(b \leq a\), any future price that
problem.md:248:exceeds \(a\) also exceeds \(b\). Therefore, if quote \(a\) trades within
problem.md:249:\(L_t\) steps, then quote \(b\) also trades within \(L_t\) steps. Equivalently,
problem.md:250:\[
problem.md:251:    \ind{\tau_t(a) \leq L_t}
problem.md:252:    \leq
problem.md:253:    \ind{\tau_t(b) \leq L_t}
problem.md:254:\]
problem.md:255:on every realized path. Hence, pathwise,
problem.md:256:\[
problem.md:257:\begin{aligned}
problem.md:258:    r^H_t(a)
problem.md:259:    &=
problem.md:260:    a\cdot \ind{\tau_t(a) \leq L_t} \\
problem.md:261:    &=
problem.md:262:    b\cdot \ind{\tau_t(a) \leq L_t}
problem.md:263:    +
problem.md:264:    (a-b)\cdot \ind{\tau_t(a) \leq L_t} \\
problem.md:265:    &\leq
problem.md:266:    b\cdot \ind{\tau_t(b) \leq L_t}
problem.md:267:    +
problem.md:268:    (a-b) \\
problem.md:269:    &\leq
problem.md:270:    r^H_t(b) + \frac{1}{K}.
problem.md:271:\end{aligned}
problem.md:272:\]
problem.md:273:Taking expectations and summing over \(t\), we get
problem.md:274:\[
problem.md:275:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:276:    \leq
problem.md:277:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(b(a))\right]
problem.md:278:    +
problem.md:279:    \frac{T}{K}
problem.md:280:    \leq
problem.md:281:    \max_{b \in \mathcal{G}_K}
problem.md:282:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(b)\right]
problem.md:283:    +
problem.md:284:    \frac{T}{K}.
problem.md:285:\]
problem.md:286:Since this holds for every \(a \in [0,1]\), the best continuum comparator is
problem.md:287:within \(T/K\) of the best grid comparator:
problem.md:288:\[
problem.md:289:    \max_{a \in [0,1]}
problem.md:290:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a)\right]
problem.md:291:    \leq
problem.md:292:    \max_{b \in \mathcal{G}_K}
problem.md:293:    \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(b)\right]
problem.md:294:    +
problem.md:295:    \frac{T}{K}.
problem.md:296:\]
problem.md:297:Subtracting the learner's expected truncated reward
problem.md:298:\(\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=1}^T r^H_t(a_t)\right]\) from both sides yields
problem.md:299:\[
problem.md:300:    R^H(T) \leq R^H_K(T) + \frac{T}{K}.
problem.md:301:\]
problem.md:302:\end{proof}
problem.md:303:
problem.md:304:\paragraph{Putting the terms together.}
problem.md:305:The proof sketch above began by pretending that the problem already has \(K\)
problem.md:306:arms. Lemma~\ref{lem:discretization-comparison} explains how to justify this:
problem.md:307:run the learner on the grid \(\mathcal{G}_K\), and then compare its performance
problem.md:308:to the original comparator. If the delayed finite-arm algorithm
problem.md:309:guarantees
problem.md:310:\[
problem.md:311:    R^H_K(T) = O\left(\sqrt{T H \log K}\right),
problem.md:312:\]
problem.md:313:then Lemmas~\ref{lem:truncation-comparison}
problem.md:314:and~\ref{lem:discretization-comparison} give
problem.md:315:\[
problem.md:316:\begin{aligned}
problem.md:317:    R(T)
problem.md:318:    &\leq
problem.md:319:    R^H(T) + T\beta(H) \\
problem.md:320:    &\leq
problem.md:321:    R^H_K(T) + \frac{T}{K} + T\beta(H) \\
problem.md:322:    &=
problem.md:323:    O\left(\sqrt{T H \log K}\right)
problem.md:324:    +
problem.md:325:    \frac{T}{K}
problem.md:326:    +
problem.md:327:    T\beta(H).
problem.md:328:\end{aligned}
problem.md:329:\]
problem.md:330:Thus the regret bound has three terms:
problem.md:331:\[
problem.md:332:    \underbrace{O\left(\sqrt{T H \log K}\right)}_{\text{learning with delay}}
problem.md:333:    +
problem.md:334:    \underbrace{\frac{T}{K}}_{\text{discretization}}
problem.md:335:    +
problem.md:336:    \underbrace{T\beta(H)}_{\text{truncation}}.
problem.md:337:\]
problem.md:338:The discretization term is explicitly \(T/K\).
problem.md:339:
problem.md:340:The parameters \(K\) and \(H\) should be chosen to balance these three terms.
problem.md:341:For a fixed \(H\), the grid should be fine enough that \(T/K\) does not
problem.md:342:dominate the delayed-learning term. Since \(K\) appears only logarithmically in
problem.md:343:the delayed-learning term, a simple safe choice is \(K=T\), which makes
problem.md:344:\(T/K=1\) and gives
problem.md:345:\[
problem.md:346:    R(T)
problem.md:347:    \leq
problem.md:348:    O\left(\sqrt{T H \log T}\right)
problem.md:349:    +
problem.md:350:    T\beta(H)
problem.md:351:    +
problem.md:352:    1.
problem.md:353:\]
problem.md:354:A more economical choice, up to logarithmic factors, is to take
problem.md:355:\(K \approx \sqrt{T/H}\), which makes the discretization term no larger than
problem.md:356:the delayed-learning term.
problem.md:357:
problem.md:358:After \(K\) is chosen, \(H\) controls the tradeoff between the delayed-learning
problem.md:359:term and the truncation term. Increasing \(H\) makes the finite-arm delayed
problem.md:360:learning problem harder, because the term \(\sqrt{T H \log K}\) grows with
problem.md:361:\(H\). But increasing \(H\) also makes the truncation error \(T\beta(H)\)
problem.md:362:smaller. The best choice is therefore obtained by approximately solving
problem.md:363:\[
problem.md:364:    \sqrt{T H(T) \log K(T)}
problem.md:365:    \approx
problem.md:366:    T\beta(H(T)).
problem.md:367:\]
problem.md:368:Equivalently, with the simple choice \(K=T\), one balances
problem.md:369:\[
problem.md:370:    \beta(H(T))
problem.md:371:    \approx
problem.md:372:    \sqrt{\frac{H(T)\log T}{T}}.
problem.md:373:\]
problem.md:374:There is no universal optimal \(H(T)\) without more information about the decay
problem.md:375:of \(\beta\). If \(\beta(h) \lesssim h^{-\alpha}\), then the balancing choice
problem.md:376:with \(K=T\) is
problem.md:377:\[
problem.md:378:    H(T) \asymp \left(\frac{T}{\log T}\right)^{1/(2\alpha+1)},
problem.md:379:\]
problem.md:380:which gives regret of order
problem.md:381:\[
problem.md:382:    T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}
problem.md:383:\]
problem.md:384:up to constants. If instead \(\beta(h)\) decays exponentially, then \(H(T)\)
problem.md:385:only needs to grow logarithmically in \(T\), giving regret nearly
problem.md:386:\(\sqrt{T}\) up to logarithmic factors.
problem.md:387:
problem.md:388:For the qualitative sublinear-regret result, Assumption~\ref{ass:tail-delays}
problem.md:389:is enough. Choose \(H(T)\to\infty\) slowly enough that
problem.md:390:\(H(T)\log T=o(T)\), and take \(K=T\). Then
problem.md:391:\(\sqrt{T H(T)\log T}=o(T)\), \(T\beta(H(T))=o(T)\), and \(T/K=1\).
problem.md:392:
problem.md:393:
problem.md:394:\medskip
problem.md:395:\noindent\emph{Only if direction.}
problem.md:396:\end{proof}
problem.md:397:
problem.md:398:\end{document}
