iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:I found no fatal gap in the explorer’s audit under the literal per-process reading. The sparse vanishing-spike construction does refute the stated “only if”: \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), while the always-zero learner has \(O(1)\) regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:5:The main caveat is quantifiers. This is not a minimax lower-bound refutation unless the conjecture is explicitly about individual process learnability.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:7:## Issue List
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:9:- **Missing assumption:** The refutation needs the conjecture interpreted as: for a fixed price process, existence of some sublinear-regret learner implies \(\beta(h)\to0\). If instead “possible” means a uniform guarantee over a process class, the counterexample only exposes a quantifier mismatch.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:11:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The comparator bound should explicitly handle the case where \(m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}\) does not exist. Then no spike can exceed \(a\), so the comparator payoff is \(0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:12:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:13:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The statement \(\tau_{s_n}(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n\) should be “almost surely,” since a uniform variable on \([0,1]\) can equal \(0\) on a null event. This does not affect the essential-supremum beta calculation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:14:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:15:- **Worth pursuing:** The generalized construction with \(P_{s_n}=c_nU_n/s_n\), \(0<c_n\le C\), is valid if the \(U_n\)’s are independent and \(s_{n+1}-s_n\to\infty\). It gives regret at most \(C\), hence can beat any diverging sublinear envelope eventually. It does not literally handle arbitrary sublinear envelopes that stay bounded below \(C\) or tend to \(0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:16:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:17:- **Unsupported citation:** None for the central counterexample. The delayed-learning citations are background for the upper-bound side, not needed for the refutation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:18:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:19:## Counterexamples Or Stress Tests
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:20:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:21:- **Action-space stress test:** If \(a=0\) is removed but actions arbitrarily close to \(0\) remain, the same pathology persists because \(\sup_a\) can approach the zero-action tail. If the action space is restricted to \([\delta,1]\), this particular counterexample no longer works.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:22:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:23:- **Posting-time stress test:** A quote posted at \(t=s_{m^*}\) cannot trade on the current spike, only future ones. The explorer correctly accounts for this; only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can contribute.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:24:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:25:- **Finite-horizon stress test:** For each fixed \(T\), only finitely many spikes matter, so \(F_T(a)\) is continuous and the comparator maximum exists.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:26:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:27:## Literature Or Known-Result Conflicts
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:28:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:29:No conflict found. The standard delayed-feedback literature supports the upper-bound intuition but does not imply the unweighted \(\beta\)-tail condition is necessary.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:30:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:31:Joulani, György, and Szepesvári give black-box delayed-feedback reductions and note different delay penalties in adversarial versus stochastic settings. Quanrud and Khashabi give adversarial-delay regret bounds in terms of total delay. These are consistent with using truncation/skipping ideas, but they do not rule out payoff-negligible long delays.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:32:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:33:## What Survives The Critique
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:35:The following claims survive:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:36:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:37:- The sparse-spike process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:38:- The best fixed comparator has expected cumulative reward at most \(1\) for every horizon.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:39:- The always-zero learner therefore has constant regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:40:- The stated unweighted tail condition is sufficient-looking but not necessary under the literal individual-process interpretation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:41:- A payoff-weighted or horizon-level truncation condition is a more plausible next target.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:42:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:43:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:44:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:45:- Pooria Joulani, András György, Csaba Szepesvári. “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback.” ICML/PMLR 2013. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html. Relevant for delayed-feedback reductions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:46:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:47:- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi. “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays.” NeurIPS 2015. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html. Relevant for regret bounds in terms of cumulative delay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:48:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:49:- Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich. “On delayed prediction of individual sequences.” IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 48(7):1959-1976, 2002. DOI: 10.1109/TIT.2002.1013136. Relevant as early delayed prediction background.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:50:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:51:- Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi, Tommaso Cesari, Roberto Colomboni, Luigi Foscari, Vinayak Pathak. “Market Making without Regret.” COLT/PMLR 2025. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/cesa-bianchi25a.html. Relevant market-making regret background.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:52:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:53:## Recommended Next Checks
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:54:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:55:1. Formalize the sparse-spike construction as a proposition with explicit quantifiers.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:56:2. Decide whether the target theorem is individual-process, class-uniform, or minimax.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:57:3. Test payoff-weighted \(\gamma(h)\) and horizon-level \(\Delta_T(H)\) against rare-history and predictable-spike examples.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:58:4. For any necessity claim, require long delays at positive payoff scale plus an indistinguishability argument.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:3:The sparse vanishing-spike refutation checks out under the literal per-process reading of the conjecture. It gives a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\), yet the learner that always posts \(0\) has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. So the stated “only if” direction is false as written.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:5:The core flaw exposed is that \(\beta\) is an unweighted delay-tail condition: it counts long delays for quotes whose payoff scale is negligible, especially \(a=0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:7:## Concrete Progress
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:9:For \(s_n=2^n\), \(U_n\sim \mathrm{Unif}(0,1)\), and
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:10:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:11:P_t=\begin{cases}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:12:U_n/s_n,&t=s_n,\\
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:13:0,&\text{otherwise},
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:14:\end{cases}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:15:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:16:I verified:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:17:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:18:- \(P_t\in[0,1]\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:19:- For \(a=0\) and \(t=s_n\), \(\tau_t(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n=2^n\) almost surely.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:20:- Hence for every \(h\), choosing \(n\) with \(2^n>h\) gives \(\beta(h)=1\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:21:- For every fixed \(a>0\), if \(m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}\), then only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can possibly earn reward, so
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:22:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:23:\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<1.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:24:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:25:- For \(a=0\), reward is identically \(0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:26:- Therefore \(\max_a \mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\le 1\), while the always-zero learner earns \(0\). Regret is \(O(1)\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:27:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:28:## Claims Or Lemmas
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:29:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:30:**Lemma 1, audited valid.** The sparse-spike process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:31:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:32:**Lemma 2, audited valid.** The best fixed comparator has expected cumulative reward at most \(1\) for every horizon \(T\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:33:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:34:**Lemma 3, audited valid.** The comparator maximum exists. For fixed \(T\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:35:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:36:F_T(a)=\sum_{t=1}^T a\,\Pr\!\left[\max_{m:t<s_m\le T} U_m/s_m>a\right]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:37:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:38:is a finite sum of continuous functions on \([0,1]\), including continuity at \(a=0\), so \(F_T\) attains its maximum.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:39:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:40:**Strengthening.** More generally, take unbounded gaps \(s_{n+1}-s_n\to\infty\) and
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:41:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:42:P_{s_n}=c_n U_n/s_n,\qquad P_t=0\text{ otherwise},
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:43:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:44:with \(0<c_n\le C\). Then \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(C\). Thus the counterexample can be scaled to have arbitrarily small constant regret. In particular, it is below any diverging sublinear regret envelope eventually.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:45:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:46:## Proof Attempts
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:47:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:48:The \(\beta\)-calculation is sound because the definition takes \(\sup_a\), and \(a=0\) is allowed. Conditioning on \(P_1,\dots,P_{s_n}\) does not reveal \(U_{n+1}\), but \(U_{n+1}>0\) almost surely, so the next positive price occurs exactly at \(s_{n+1}\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:49:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:50:The comparator bound is also sound. The solver’s argument uses only the maximum possible spike height, not the exact trade probability, so it is a valid upper bound. It correctly handles the fact that a quote posted at \(t=s_{m^*}\) cannot trade on the current spike, only on future prices.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:51:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:52:The continuum-max issue is not a gap in this construction because the spike heights have continuous distributions and only finitely many spikes appear before any fixed horizon.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:53:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:54:## Gaps And Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:55:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:56:This refutes necessity only for the literal per-process statement. If the intended theorem is minimax over a class of processes, the quantifiers need to be rewritten before this becomes a full refutation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:57:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:58:The example exploits vanishing payoff scale. If the action space were restricted to \([\delta,1]\), this particular obstruction would disappear. If \(0\) is removed but arbitrarily small positive actions remain and \(\sup_a\) is still used, the same pathology persists as a supremum phenomenon.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:59:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:60:The “if” direction still depends on formalizing the reduction to full-information delayed expert learning, but that is separate from this refutation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:61:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:62:## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:63:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:64:The sparse vanishing-spike process is a decisive obstruction to the stated iff. Long finite hitting times can occur with probability one for actions whose reward is zero or arbitrarily small, so unweighted tail decay is too strong as a necessary condition.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:65:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:66:A better candidate condition should weight the tail by payoff, e.g.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:67:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:68:\gamma(h)=\sup_{t,a}\operatorname*{ess\,sup} a\,\Pr[h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{1:t}],
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:69:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:70:or use a horizon-level comparator-weighted truncation error.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:71:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:72:## Sources Consulted
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:73:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:74:- Joulani, György, Szepesvári, “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback,” ICML/PMLR 2013. Used only for delayed-feedback reduction context. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:75:- Quanrud, Khashabi, “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays,” NeurIPS 2015. Used for regret bounds in terms of total delay. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:76:- Weinberger, Ordentlich, “On delayed prediction of individual sequences,” IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 48(7):1959-1976, 2002. Checked as background delayed-prediction literature.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:77:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:78:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:79:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:80:- Pooria Joulani, András György, Csaba Szepesvári. “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback.” ICML 2013, PMLR 28(3):1453-1461. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html Relevant for black-box delayed online-learning reductions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:81:- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi. “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays.” NeurIPS 2015. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html Relevant for delayed-feedback regret bounds via total delay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:82:- Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich. “On delayed prediction of individual sequences.” IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 48(7):1959-1976, 2002. DOI: 10.1109/TIT.2002.1013136. Relevant as early delayed prediction/reference-strategy work.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:83:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:84:## Recommended Next Steps
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:85:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_01.md:86:Formalize the sparse-spike counterexample as a numbered proposition in the ledger. Then redirect the necessity search toward payoff-weighted tail conditions; the current \(\beta\)-condition is sufficient but not necessary.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:3:The explorer’s core claim survives: the sparse vanishing-spike construction is a valid refutation of the “only if” direction under the literal per-process reading. I found no fatal gap in \(\beta(h)=1\), the \(O(1)\) comparator bound, or the always-zero learner argument.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:5:The main weaknesses are scope and polish: the result depends on the interpretation of “possible,” the envelope strengthening needs a finite-prefix fix, and the “rare bad histories” obstruction is only a conjectural direction.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:7:## Issue List
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:9:- **Worth pursuing:** Core sparse-spike refutation. For \(a=0\), at spike time \(s_n=2^n\), the next positive price occurs at \(s_{n+1}\) almost surely, so \(\tau_{s_n}(0)=2^n\). Thus \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:11:- **Worth pursuing:** Comparator bound. If fixed \(a>0\) can trade by horizon \(T\), then \(a < 1/s_{m^*}\) for the last possible crossing spike, and at most \(s_{m^*}\) posted quotes can earn. Hence total expected reward is \(< a s_{m^*}<1\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:12:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:13:- **Missing assumption:** The refutation only proves failure of instance-wise necessity:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:14:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:15:  \exists \pi:\ R_P^\pi(T)=o(T)\quad\not\Rightarrow\quad \beta_P(h)\to0.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:16:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:17:  It does not refute a minimax theorem over a process class unless the class-uniform learner/regret quantifiers are stated.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:18:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:19:- **Plausible but incomplete:** Envelope strengthening needs a finite-prefix repair. If \(q\) is only eventually positive/nondecreasing, choose spike times after that threshold and set, for example,
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:20:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:21:  c_n=\min\{1,q(s_n)/4\}>0.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:22:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:23:  As written, early \(c_n\)'s could violate the claimed \(q(T)/2\) bound for bounded envelopes.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:24:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:25:- **Plausible but incomplete:** Continuum maximum proof is basically right, but should explicitly use “no positive atoms” of \(M_t\). The possible atom at \(0\) is harmless because the objective has the factor \(a\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:26:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:27:- **Missing assumption:** The obstruction relies on \(0\in[0,1]\), reward scale \(a\), and strict crossing \(P>a\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), this exact example no longer forces \(\beta(h)=1\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:28:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:29:- **Unsupported claim:** “Rare bad histories can keep \(\beta\) large without contributing linear expected regret” is plausible but not proved by this construction. Keep it as a direction, not a lemma.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:30:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:31:## Counterexamples Or Stress Tests
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:32:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:33:- Restricting to \(a\ge\epsilon\) kills this example: after \(s_m>1/\epsilon\), no spike can exceed \(\epsilon\). This confirms the flaw is the unweighted inclusion of vanishing payoff actions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:35:- Worst horizons \(T=s_m\) still satisfy the comparator bound; there is no hidden growth at spike horizons.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:36:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:37:- The construction needs both long gaps and vanishing heights: long gaps give \(\beta=1\), while vanishing heights keep comparator payoff bounded.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:38:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:39:## Literature Or Known-Result Conflicts
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:40:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:41:No conflict found. Joulani-Gyorgy-Szepesvari supports the delayed-feedback reduction behind the sufficient direction, not the necessity claim. Thune-Cesa-Bianchi-Seldin and Zimmert-Seldin support the broader point that unrestricted delays are often handled by total-delay or skip-set quantities, not uniform worst-case tail decay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:42:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:43:## What Survives The Critique
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:44:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:45:- Sparse vanishing spikes refute unweighted \(\beta\)-necessity under individual-law learnability.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:46:- The always-zero learner has \(R(T)\le1=o(T)\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:47:- \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:48:- The right next target is a payoff-weighted, horizon-averaged, or minimax-information condition.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:49:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:50:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:51:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:52:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari, “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback,” ICML/PMLR 2013. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html Relevant for bounded/total delayed-feedback reductions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:53:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:54:- Tobias Sommer Thune, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin, “Nonstochastic Multiarmed Bandits with Unrestricted Delays,” NeurIPS 2019. https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/hash/0e4f5cc9f4f3f7f1651a6b9f9214e5b1-Abstract.html Relevant for skipping excessive delays.
