iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:110:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:111:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:112:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:113:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari, “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback,” ICML 2013, PMLR 28(3):1453-1461. Relevant for the delayed-feedback reduction behind the sufficient direction. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:114:- Tobias Sommer Thune, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin, “Nonstochastic Multiarmed Bandits with Unrestricted Delays,” NeurIPS 2019. Relevant for refined conditions with unrestricted delays and skipped excessive delays. https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/hash/0e4f5cc9f4f3f7f1651a6b9f9214e5b1-Abstract.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:115:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:116:## Recommended Next Steps
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:117:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:118:Restate the target theorem with explicit quantifiers over process classes and learner dependence.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:119:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:120:Replace unweighted \(\beta\) by a payoff-weighted or horizon-level condition. The sparse-spike example has \(\beta\equiv1\) but satisfies the heuristic bound \(a\Pr[h<\tau_t(a)<\infty]\lesssim 1/h\), which is why it is easy despite maximal unweighted tail failure.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:3:The explorer’s main sparse-vanishing-spike refutation appears correct under the literal per-process reading of the conjecture and the action set \([0,1]\). I do not see a fatal gap in the proof that \(\beta(h)=1\) while the always-zero learner has \(O(1)\) regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:5:The main limitation is scope: this only refutes an individual-law necessity statement. It does not refute a minimax/class-uniform theorem unless the quantifiers are made explicit.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:7:## Issue List
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:9:- **Worth pursuing:** The core counterexample is valid. At \(t=2^n\), action \(a=0\) waits exactly \(2^n\) steps for the next positive spike almost surely, so \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:11:- **Worth pursuing:** The comparator bound is sound. For fixed \(a>0\), only spikes with \(a<1/2^m\) can ever trigger reward. If \(m^*\) is the last such spike before \(T\), then pathwise the total comparator reward is at most \(a\,2^{m^*}<1\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:12:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:13:- **Missing assumption:** The refutation relies on the conjecture being interpreted per process:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:14:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:15:  \exists \pi\ R^\pi_P(T)=o(T) \Rightarrow \beta_P(h)\to0.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:16:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:17:  If “possible” means one algorithm works uniformly over a process class, the counterexample is not decisive.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:18:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:19:- **Missing assumption:** The exact proof uses \(a=0\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), this construction no longer violates the tail condition. If actions are \((0,1]\), a small modification still gives \(\limsup_h\beta(h)>0\), but not \(\beta(h)=1\) by the same argument.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:20:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:21:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The envelope strengthening is likely correct, but it should explicitly define \(c_n=\min\{1,q(s_n)/2\}\) for large \(n\), keep \(c_n>0\), and state the result only for sufficiently large horizons.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:22:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:23:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The payoff-weighted tail suggestion is useful for sufficiency, but no necessity claim should be inferred from it. Predictable sparse high-value delays could still make weighted tails large while allowing low regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:24:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:25:## Counterexamples Or Stress Tests
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:26:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:27:- **Lower-bounded action set:** If the action set is \([\epsilon,1]\), then eventually all spikes are below \(\epsilon\). For large enough \(h\), no action has a long finite hitting time, so this counterexample disappears.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:28:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:29:- **Open-at-zero action set:** If \(0\) is excluded but arbitrarily small actions are allowed, take \(a=1/(2s_{n+1})\) at \(t=s_n\). Then the next spike exceeds \(a\) with probability \(1/2\), so \(\beta(h)\) stays bounded away from zero. The bounded-regret argument still works.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:30:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:31:- **Quantifier stress test:** A class containing many hidden sparse-spike processes may still be hard for a single learner. The explorer only proves one benign law with bad \(\beta\), not a class-uniform learnability result.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:32:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:33:## Literature Or Known-Result Conflicts
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:35:No conflict found. The delayed-feedback references support the sufficiency-side technology, not the explorer’s counterexample directly.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:36:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:37:Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari give black-box delayed-feedback reductions for online learning, relevant to the \(O(\sqrt{TH\log K})\) style bound. Thune, Cesa-Bianchi, and Seldin study unrestricted delays and skipping large delays, which is conceptually aligned with truncation but in a bandit setting.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:38:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:39:## What Survives The Critique
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:40:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:41:The sparse-spike construction should be recorded as a genuine obstruction to the stated “only if” direction.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:42:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:43:The important diagnosis survives: unweighted worst-case delay tails are too strong because they can be driven by negligible-payoff actions and sparse time locations.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:44:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:45:## Bibliography Candidates
