iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:154:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:155:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:156:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:157:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari. “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback.” ICML 2013, PMLR 28(3):1453-1461. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html. Relevant for black-box delayed-feedback reductions; constant delay \(H\) yields the needed expert-style \(O(\sqrt{TH\log K})\) scaling.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:158:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:159:- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi. “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays.” NeurIPS 2015. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html. Relevant background for regret controlled by total delay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:160:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:161:- Lukas Zierahn, Dirk van der Hoeven, Tal Lancewicki, Aviv Rosenberg, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi. “A Unified Analysis of Nonstochastic Delayed Feedback for Combinatorial Semi-Bandits, Linear Bandits, and MDPs.” JMLR 26(104):1-60, 2025. https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v26/24-0496.html. Modern delayed-feedback reference; less directly needed because the truncated problem here is full-information.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:162:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:163:## Recommended Next Steps
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:164:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:165:Promote \(\gamma(h)\to0\) as a sufficient condition theorem.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:166:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:167:For necessity, first fix the intended quantifier. If process-specific, record the counterexample above and abandon necessity of uniform \(\gamma\). If class-uniform/minimax, try a two-process indistinguishability lower bound where delayed positive-payoff events hide which of two separated quotes is better.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:168:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:169:Also consider replacing uniform essential-sup \(\gamma\) by the ex-ante average tail \(\Delta_T(H)\), which is closer to what the truncation proof actually needs.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:1:# Payoff-Weighted Tail
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:3:ID: idea_02
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:5:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:6:Replace beta by the payoff-weighted tail gamma(h)=sup_t sup_a esssup a Pr(h<tau_t(a)<infty | history). Prove the same upper bound with T gamma(H) in place of T beta(H), and show equivalence to uniform tightness over positive-scale actions a>=epsilon for every epsilon>0. Then test whether gamma(h)->0 is necessary or only sufficient.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:7:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:8:## Why Promising
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:9:It directly repairs the solver's obstruction: long delays at a=0 or tiny quote levels should not matter for regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:11:## Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:12:The essential supremum over histories may still be too strong; rare histories or isolated times can violate gamma without causing linear expected regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:13:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:14:## Suggested Lenses
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:15:- proof strategy
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:16:- condition refinement
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md:17:- counterexample search
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:3:The payoff-weighted tail condition is a valid sufficient refinement:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:5:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:6:\gamma(h)=\sup_{t,a}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:7:a\,\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid \mathcal F_t)\to 0.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:8:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:9:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:10:It gives the same truncation proof with \(T\gamma(H)\) replacing \(T\beta(H)\). It is also exactly equivalent to uniform tail tightness on every positive action scale \(a\ge \epsilon\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:11:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:12:However, \(\gamma(h)\to0\) still looks too strong as a necessary condition. The essential supremum over histories and the supremum over times can fail because of rare or sparse exceptional histories whose total payoff contribution is \(o(T)\). A more natural condition for expected regret is a horizon-averaged payoff tail.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:13:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:14:## Concrete Progress
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:15:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:16:Define the averaged truncation mass
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:17:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:18:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:19:\Delta_T(H)
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:20:=
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:21:\sup_{a\in[0,1]}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:22:\sum_{t=1}^T
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:23:\mathbb E\left[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:24:a\,\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:25:\right].
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:26:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:27:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:28:Then the same argument gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:29:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:30:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:31:R(T)\le
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:32:O(\sqrt{T H\log K})+\frac{T}{K}+\Delta_T(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:33:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:35:Thus sublinear regret follows whenever there is \(H_T\) with \(H_T\log T=o(T)\) and \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\), taking for instance \(K=T\). The condition \(\gamma(H_T)\to0\) implies this because \(\Delta_T(H_T)\le T\gamma(H_T)\), but it is strictly stronger.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:36:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:37:## Claims Or Lemmas
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:38:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:39:**Lemma 1: Payoff-weighted truncation.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:40:For fixed \(H\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:41:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:42:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:43:R(T)\le R^H(T)+T\gamma(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:44:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:45:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:46:Reason: \(r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)=a\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}\le a\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:47:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:48:**Lemma 2: Positive-scale equivalence.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:49:Let
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:50:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:51:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:52:Q_\epsilon(h)=
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:53:\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:54:\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:55:\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid \mathcal F_t).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:56:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:57:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:58:Then \(\gamma(h)\to0\) iff \(Q_\epsilon(h)\to0\) for every \(\epsilon>0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:59:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:60:Proof sketch: \(Q_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\). Conversely,
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:61:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:62:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:63:\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+Q_\epsilon(h),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:64:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:65:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:66:since the contribution of \(a<\epsilon\) is at most \(\epsilon\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:67:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:68:**Lemma 3: Averaged condition suffices.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:69:The bound with \(\Delta_T(H)\) above proves sublinear regret under the averaged condition. This is the better condition-refinement target if the goal is expected regret from the initial distribution rather than uniform guarantees after every reachable history.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:70:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:71:## Proof Attempts
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:72:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:73:The upper-bound proof survives the beta-to-gamma replacement without structural changes. Discretization is unchanged: rounding \(a\) down to a grid point loses at most \(T/K\). The finite-grid truncated problem has bounded delay \(H\), and because prices are revealed, counterfactual truncated rewards for all grid quotes become known after \(H\) steps.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:74:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:75:The necessity attempt for \(\gamma\) breaks: failure of an essential-supremum condition need not create linear expected regret. It only says that somewhere, after some history, a positive quote has a long conditional tail. It does not say that such histories occur often enough, or that late payoff mass is hard to predict.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:76:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:77:## Gaps And Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:78:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:79:The original statement uses \(\max_{a\in[0,1]}\), but with strict trade condition \(P>a\), maxima may fail to exist; often only a supremum exists. The discretization proof already behaves like a supremum argument.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:80:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:81:The delayed-expert bound should be cited carefully as full-information delayed feedback, not bandit feedback, since the revealed price path lets us compute all grid arms’ truncated outcomes.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:82:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:83:A true “only if” theorem needs a precise quantifier structure: instance-wise learnability, minimax over a process class, or uniform post-history learnability. Tail mass alone is not a hardness condition.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:84:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:85:## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:86:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:87:Sparse deterministic obstruction to necessity of \(\gamma\): take epochs with \(n\) zeros followed by \(n^3\) ones. For any \(h\), choose \(n>h\) and the first zero in that block. For \(a=1/2\), the next trade occurs after more than \(h\) steps with probability \(1\), so \(\gamma(h)\ge 1/2\) for all \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:88:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:89:But the total number of zero-block positions through epoch \(N\) is \(O(N^2)\), while total time is \(O(N^4)\). Hence the averaged tail mass is \(o(T)\), and the averaged truncation condition gives sublinear regret. This shows \(\sup_t\) is too strong for expected-regret necessity.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:90:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:91:Rare-history variants give the same obstruction to the essential supremum: a very low-probability branch can contain arbitrarily long positive-payoff delays, making \(\gamma\) fail while contributing negligible expected regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:92:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:93:## Sources Consulted
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:94:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:95:Local: `problem.md`, all requested `working_notes/*.md`, and `iterations/iter_001/idea_02/idea.md`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_02.md:96:
