iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:79:## Recommended Next Checks
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:80:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:81:1. Formalize the spike counterexample as a proposition with an all-horizons regret bound.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:82:2. Decide whether the target theorem is instance-level, Bayesian/process-aware, or uniform/minimax.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:83:3. State the delayed-grid feedback model exactly and cite a full-information delayed-experts theorem.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_02.md:84:4. If pursuing necessity, try a two-environment lower bound where histories agree up to delay `H` but the optimal fixed quote differs because of unresolved tail behavior.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:1:# Horizon Tail Error
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:3:ID: idea_03
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:5:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:6:Study the ex-ante horizon-level truncation error Delta_T(H)=sup_a sum_{t<=T} E[a 1{H<tau_t(a)<=T-t}]. Establish sufficiency when some H_T has H_T log T=o(T) and Delta_T(H_T)=o(T), using the same delayed-grid reduction. Investigate whether this is closer to a true characterization than uniform gamma.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:7:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:8:## Why Promising
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:9:This is the weakest condition visible from the truncation proof and avoids beta's worst-case sup over irrelevant times and histories.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:11:## Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:12:Necessity may fail because an algorithm can exploit predictable price structure rather than waiting for delayed reward feedback.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:13:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:14:## Suggested Lenses
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:15:- proof strategy
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:16:- necessity test
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md:17:- rare-history examples
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:3:For `idea_03`, the horizon-tail condition is a clean **sufficiency** condition, but it is not a genuine **necessity** condition for instance-level learnability.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:5:The obstruction is not technical: large long-horizon tail reward can be perfectly predictable or distributionally known, so the learner may not need delayed reward feedback to choose the right quote. I found a concrete counterexample where `Delta_T(H_T)` is linear along infinitely many horizons for every `H_T log T = o(T)`, while a fixed learner has `o(T)` regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:7:## Concrete Progress
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:9:The proposed replacement tail quantity is
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:11:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:12:\Delta_T(H)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:13:=
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:14:\sup_{a\in[0,1]}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:15:\sum_{t\le T}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:16:\mathbb E\left[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:17:a\,\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:18:\right].
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:19:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:20:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:21:The same truncation proof gives the sharper sufficient bound
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:22:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:23:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:24:R(T)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:25:\lesssim
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:26:\sqrt{T H\log K}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:27:+
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:28:T/K
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:29:+
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:30:\Delta_T(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:31:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:32:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:33:Thus if some `H_T` satisfies `H_T log T = o(T)` and `Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)`, then choosing for instance `K=T` gives sublinear regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:35:But the reverse implication fails without an additional “unpredictability” or “model-free” requirement.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:36:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:37:## Claims Or Lemmas
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:38:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:39:**Lemma 1, sufficiency via horizon-tail error.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:40:For the grid learner run on rewards truncated at delay `H`,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:41:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:42:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:43:R(T)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:44:\le
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:45:R_K^H(T)+T/K+\Delta_T(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:46:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:47:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:48:This is exactly the existing truncation/discretization argument with `T beta(H)` replaced by the ex ante comparator tail loss `Delta_T(H)`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:49:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:50:**Lemma 2, non-necessity counterexample.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:51:There exists a price process with `Delta_T(H_T)=Omega(T)` along infinitely many horizons for every `H_T log T=o(T)`, but with an explicit learner whose regret is `o(T)`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:52:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:53:## Proof Attempts
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:54:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:55:Counterexample construction.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:56:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:57:Let spike times grow superexponentially, for example
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:58:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:59:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:60:S_n = 2^{2^n}.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:61:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:62:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:63:Let `U_n` be i.i.d. `Uniform[0,1]`. Define
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:64:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:65:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:66:P_t =
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:67:\begin{cases}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:68:U_n, & t=S_n,\\
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:69:0, & \text{otherwise}.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:70:\end{cases}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:71:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:72:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:73:Consider the learner that always posts
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:74:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:75:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:76:a_t = 1/2.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:77:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:78:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:79:At horizon `T=S_n`, quotes posted in the last block `(S_{n-1}, S_n)` have exactly one chance to trade before the horizon, namely at `S_n`. For a fixed quote `a`, the expected reward on each such quote is
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:80:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:81:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:82:a\,\Pr(U_n>a)=a(1-a),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:83:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:84:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:85:maximized at `a=1/2`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:86:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:87:The previous blocks contain only `S_{n-1}=o(S_n)` quotes total. Hence, even if the best fixed comparator extracts the maximum possible extra reward from all older blocks, its advantage over the always-`1/2` learner is at most `o(T)`. Between spike horizons, no new dominant block has resolved, so the same domination argument gives `o(T)` regret for all horizons.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:88:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:89:However, for `T=S_n` and any `H_T=o(T)`, the quotes in the last block with posting times at least `H_T` before `S_n` contribute to the tail error. Taking `a=1/2`,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:90:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:91:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:92:\Delta_T(H_T)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:93:\ge
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:94:\frac14\,(S_n-S_{n-1}-H_T)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:95:=
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:96:\Omega(T).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:97:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:98:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:99:So the horizon-tail condition fails strongly, even though sublinear regret is achievable.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:100:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:101:## Gaps And Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:102:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:103:This counterexample refutes necessity only under an **instance-level/existence** interpretation of “sublinear regret is possible.”
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:104:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:105:If the intended theorem is instead minimax or distribution-free over a class of processes unknown to the learner, then the counterexample is not decisive because the learner hard-codes the useful distributional fact that the one-spike optimizer is `1/2`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:106:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:107:So the main definitional gap is:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:108:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:109:Does “possible” mean:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:110:1. for a fixed process, there exists some process-aware learner, or
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:111:2. one learner gets sublinear regret uniformly over a class of admissible processes?
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:112:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:113:Necessity might still be salvageable under the second interpretation, but not from `Delta_T(H)` alone.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:114:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:115:## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:116:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:117:The counterexample is stronger than merely saying `Delta_T` takes a supremum over irrelevant actions. In the construction above, the linear tail is carried by the asymptotically optimal quote itself.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:118:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:119:Thus even replacing `sup_a` by a tail over near-optimal comparators would not fix necessity. The missing ingredient is informational: whether the long-delayed tail contains information the learner actually needs.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:120:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:121:A more plausible necessary condition would need to measure something like **unpredictable tail mass**, or be stated for classes closed under changing the unresolved tail law while preserving the learner’s observable history.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:122:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:123:## Sources Consulted
