iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:144:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:145:## Recommended Next Steps
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:146:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:147:Clarify the theorem’s quantifiers before pursuing necessity.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:148:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_02.md:149:If the goal is instance-level characterization, `Delta_T(H)` is not necessary. If the goal is uniform learnability over a process class, try to formulate a lower bound using pairs of environments that agree on all observations available before delay `H` but have different optimal fixed quotes. That would test an informational tail condition rather than a raw reward-tail condition.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:3:The horizon-tail condition is a clean sufficiency replacement for the stronger uniform conditional tail assumption. The proof strategy is sound for an upper bound:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:5:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:6:\Delta_T(H)=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb E\left[a\,\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}\right].
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:7:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:9:If there are sequences \(H_T,K_T\) such that
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:11:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:12:H_T\log K_T=o(T),\qquad T/K_T=o(T),\qquad \Delta_T(H_T)=o(T),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:13:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:14:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:15:then the same delayed-grid reduction gives sublinear regret. With \(K_T=T\), this becomes the idea’s condition \(H_T\log T=o(T)\) and \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:16:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:17:I do not think \(\Delta_T\) is likely to be necessary without extra unpredictability assumptions. Large delayed comparator mass can be harmless when the price path is predictable or when the optimal quote is obvious without feedback.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:18:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:19:## Concrete Progress
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:20:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:21:The exact truncation comparison becomes sharper:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:22:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:23:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:24:R_T \le R_T^H+\Delta_T(H),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:25:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:26:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:27:instead of
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:28:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:29:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:30:R_T\le R_T^H+T\beta(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:31:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:32:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:33:Reason: for every fixed comparator \(a\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:35:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:36:r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:37:=
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:38:a\,\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:39:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:40:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:41:Taking expectations and maximizing over \(a\) gives the \(\Delta_T(H)\) term. The learner’s realized true reward remains pathwise at least its truncated reward, so no learner-tail term is needed.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:42:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:43:Then discretization gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:44:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:45:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:46:R_T^H \le R_{T,K}^H+T/K.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:47:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:48:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:49:For the finite grid, a simple \(H+1\)-subsequence Hedge construction gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:50:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:51:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:52:R_{T,K}^H=O\!\left(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}\right).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:53:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:54:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:55:Thus
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:56:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:57:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:58:R_T
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:59:\le
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:60:O\!\left(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}\right)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:61:+\frac{T}{K}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:62:+\Delta_T(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:63:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:64:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:65:## Claims Or Lemmas
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:66:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:67:**Lemma 1, proved modulo standard finite-expert regret.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:68:For exogenous prices and full observation of the price sequence, the above bound holds for any deterministic \(H,K\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:69:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:70:**Lemma 2, proved.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:71:The original \(\beta\)-tail condition implies the horizon-tail condition, since
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:72:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:73:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:74:\Delta_T(H)\le T\beta(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:75:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:76:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:77:The implication is strict: \(\Delta_T\) ignores rare histories and irrelevant times/actions that dominate the essential supremum in \(\beta\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:78:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:79:**Claim 3, cautionary.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:80:\(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\) is close to minimal for this particular truncation proof, but not for all possible algorithms.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:81:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:82:## Proof Attempts
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:83:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:84:A self-contained finite-delay proof can avoid invoking a heavy delayed-feedback theorem. Split times into residue classes modulo \(H+1\). On one residue class, by the next occurrence of that residue, all \(H\)-truncated reward vectors from the previous occurrence have matured. Run an independent full-information Hedge instance on each residue class.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:85:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:86:For residue class \(j\) with \(n_j\) rounds,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:87:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:88:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:89:R_j=O(\sqrt{n_j\log K}).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:90:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:91:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:92:Summing and using Cauchy-Schwarz,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:93:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:94:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:95:\sum_{j=0}^{H}R_j
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:96:\le
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:97:O\!\left(\sqrt{(H+1)T\log K}\right).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:98:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:99:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:100:This proves the delayed-grid term pathwise for arbitrary reward vectors in \([0,1]^K\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:101:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:102:## Gaps And Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:103:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:104:The theorem needs a quantifier decision: is \(H_T\) known to the learner, or only existential? If only existential, a universal algorithm over all admissible \(\Delta_T\) rates needs a model-selection argument over truncation scales.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:105:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:106:The regret definition uses \(\max_{a\in[0,1]}\), but with strict trade condition \(a<P_t\), the maximum may fail to exist when prices have atoms. The clean version should use \(\sup\), restrict actions to \([0,1)\), or change the fill rule.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:107:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:108:The proof assumes prices are exogenous. If learner quotes affect future prices, counterfactual grid rewards are not well-defined from the realized price path.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:109:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:110:## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:111:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:112:A rare-history obstruction to \(\beta\): let there be one random future spike time \(N\) with a heavy tail, and prices are low before \(N\), high at \(N\). Conditional on having survived to a very late time, the residual waiting time can be large with high probability, so the essential-supremum \(\beta(h)\) may not decay. But the ex-ante total mass before the spike can be only \(O(\log T)\), so \(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\). This supports replacing \(\beta\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:113:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:114:A necessity obstruction to \(\Delta_T\): deterministic spikes at known sparse times, e.g. powers of two. For horizons ending at a spike, any \(H=o(T)\) leaves \(\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T)\). Yet a horizon-aware learner that always quotes very close to the spike price can obtain near-comparator reward without waiting for feedback. Thus large \(\Delta_T\) alone does not imply linear regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:115:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:116:## Sources Consulted
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:117:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:118:Local files: `problem.md`, all listed `working_notes/*.md`, and `iterations/iter_001/idea_03/idea.md`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:119:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:120:External sources:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:121:- Joulani, György, Szepesvári, “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback,” ICML 2013, PMLR: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:122:- Quanrud, Khashabi, “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays,” NeurIPS 2015: https://papers.neurips.cc/paper/5833-online-learning-with-adversarial-delays.pdf
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:123:- van der Hoeven, Cesa-Bianchi, “Nonstochastic Bandits and Experts with Arm-Dependent Delays,” AISTATS 2022, PMLR: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v151/van-der-hoeven22a.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:124:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:125:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:126:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:127:- Pooria Joulani, András György, Csaba Szepesvári. “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback.” ICML 2013, PMLR 28(3):1453-1461. Relevant for black-box delayed-feedback reductions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:128:- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi. “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays.” NeurIPS 2015. Relevant for regret bounds depending on total delay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:129:- Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi. “Nonstochastic Bandits and Experts with Arm-Dependent Delays.” AISTATS 2022, PMLR 151:2022-2044. Relevant because market-making delays depend on the chosen quote.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:130:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:131:## Recommended Next Steps
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:132:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:133:Formalize the \(\Delta_T\) sufficiency proposition with `sup` rather than `max`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:134:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:135:Decide whether the upper bound is nonuniform in \(H_T\) or whether the project needs an adaptive truncation-scale algorithm.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:136:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/explorer_01.md:137:For necessity, do not try to prove it from \(\Delta_T\) alone. Add an unpredictability or indistinguishability condition, then attempt a two-environment lower bound where delayed rewards are also the information needed to identify the best quote.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s sufficiency claim is mostly sound: the bound
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:4:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:5:R_T \le O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K})+T/K+\Delta_T(H)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:6:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:7:survives critique, assuming full observation of prices, realized-path counterfactual rewards, deterministic known \(H,K\), and a `sup` comparator.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:9:The characterization/necessity direction does not survive. \(\Delta_T(H)\) is a useful sufficient truncation error, but it is not necessary without an unpredictability or information-lower-bound assumption.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:11:## Issue List
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:12:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:13:- **Worth pursuing:** The truncation comparison with \(\Delta_T(H)\) is correct. For each fixed comparator \(a\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:14:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:15:  r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)=a\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\},
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:16:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:17:  and the learner’s true reward dominates its truncated reward pathwise.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:18:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:19:- **Worth pursuing:** The residue-class finite-delay Hedge proof is valid for full-information reward vectors revealed after at most \(H\) rounds. Splitting into \(H+1\) subsequences gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:20:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:21:  O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:22:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:23:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:24:- **Missing assumption:** The theorem must say whether \(H_T,K_T\) are known to the learner. Existential \(H_T\) gives a nonuniform upper bound; a uniform theorem needs model selection, doubling, or skipping.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:25:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:26:- **Missing assumption:** The reduction needs exogenous prices or, at minimum, a realized-path regret interpretation where future prices define counterfactual rewards for all grid arms. If quotes causally affect future prices, the proof does not justify causal regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:27:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:28:- **Missing assumption:** Replace \(\max_{a\in[0,1]}\) by \(\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\), restrict actions, or change the strict fill rule. With strict \(a<P_t\), atoms can make the maximum fail to exist.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:29:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:30:- **Plausible but incomplete:** “\(\Delta_T\) is close to minimal” is true only for this truncation proof. It may be avoidable by predictable-structure algorithms, arbitrary-delay/skipping algorithms, or threshold-specific geometry.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:31:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:32:- **Unsupported citation:** The delayed-feedback papers support the generic bounded-delay expert ingredient, but they do not prove the market-making reduction unless the full-information/counterfactual-observation assumptions are checked.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:33:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:34:## Counterexamples Or Stress Tests
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:35:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:36:- **Predictable sparse spikes break necessity.** Let \(P_s=1\) at powers of two and \(0\) otherwise. At horizons \(T=2^m\), any \(H=o(T)\) gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:37:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:38:  \Delta_T(H)\ge (1-o(1))(T/2-H)=\Omega(T)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:39:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:40:  by taking \(a\) close to \(1\) over the long interval before the final spike. Yet a learner that knows the schedule and quotes \(a_t=1-1/(t+1)\) gets within \(O(\log T)\) of the fixed-action supremum. So large \(\Delta_T\) does not force linear regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:41:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:42:- **Rare-history beta separation is plausible but underspecified.** A one-spike process with \(\Pr(N=n)\asymp n^{-2}\), \(P_N=1\), and \(P_t=0\) otherwise has residual tails whose conditional essential supremum need not decay, while \(\mathbb E[N\mathbf 1\{N\le T\}]=O(\log T)\). This supports \(\Delta_T\) over \(\beta\), but the explorer should state such a concrete law.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:43:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:44:- **Atom/max pathology:** If future max price is exactly \(c\), then \(a\Pr(M>a)\) approaches \(c\) as \(a\uparrow c\) but may be zero at \(a=c\). The original `max` regret expression can be undefined.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:45:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:46:## Literature Or Known-Result Conflicts
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:47:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:48:No direct conflict found. The literature supports delayed-feedback upper-bound technology, not the necessity of any tail condition.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:49:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:50:Joulani, György, and Szepesvári give black-box delayed-feedback reductions; Quanrud and Khashabi give adversarial-delay bounds; van der Hoeven and Cesa-Bianchi handle arm-dependent delays. These are consistent with the explorer’s bounded-delay component, but none rules out predictable large-delay examples.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:51:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:52:## What Survives The Critique
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:53:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:54:The \(\Delta_T\)-based condition is a clean sufficient condition:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:55:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:56:If deterministic \(H_T,K_T\) satisfy
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:57:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:58:H_T\log K_T=o(T),\qquad T/K_T=o(T),\qquad \Delta_T(H_T)=o(T),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:59:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:60:then the delayed-grid algorithm has sublinear regret, modulo the assumptions above.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:61:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:62:What does not survive is any claim that \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\) is necessary for learnability.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:63:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:64:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:65:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:66:- Pooria Joulani, András György, Csaba Szepesvári. “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback.” ICML/PMLR 2013. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html. Relevant for delayed-feedback reductions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:67:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:68:- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi. “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays.” NeurIPS 2015. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html. Relevant for regret bounds depending on cumulative delay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:69:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:70:- Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi. “Nonstochastic Bandits and Experts with Arm-Dependent Delays.” AISTATS/PMLR 2022. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v151/van-der-hoeven22a.html. Relevant background for arm-dependent delays, though not a direct match to full-information price observation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:71:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:72:## Recommended Next Checks
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:73:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:74:1. Formalize the \(\Delta_T\) sufficiency theorem with `sup`, integer \(H\), known/nonknown \(H_T\), and explicit exogeneity/realized-path assumptions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:75:2. Add the deterministic sparse-spike example to counterexamples as a refutation of \(\Delta_T\)-necessity.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:76:3. For necessity, switch to an indistinguishability condition: delayed high-payoff mass must also be unpredictable and informationally necessary to identify the best quote.

