iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:3:The sparse vanishing-spike refutation is valid under the literal per-process reading of the conjecture. The failure is quantifier-level: \(\beta\) takes a worst-case supremum over actions, times, and histories, and therefore counts long delays for \(a=0\), even though those delays carry zero payoff and need not create regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:5:The counterexample does not refute a different minimax theorem over a specified class of processes. For that, “possible” must be restated with class-uniform algorithm and regret quantifiers.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:7:## Concrete Progress
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:9:Verified the solver’s construction:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:11:Let \(s_n=2^n\), \(U_n\sim \mathrm{Unif}(0,1)\), and
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:12:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:13:P_t =
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:14:\begin{cases}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:15:U_n/2^n, & t=2^n,\\
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:16:0, & \text{otherwise.}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:17:\end{cases}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:18:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:19:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:20:For every \(h\), choose \(n\) with \(2^n>h\). At \(t=s_n\), action \(a=0\) has
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:21:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:22:\tau_{s_n}(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n=2^n
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:23:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:24:almost surely, so
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:25:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:26:\Pr[h<\tau_{s_n}(0)<\infty\mid P_{1:s_n}]=1.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:27:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:28:Thus \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:29:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:30:For any fixed \(a>0\) and horizon \(T\), let
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:31:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:32:m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:33:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:34:If the set is empty, the comparator reward is zero. Otherwise only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can possibly trade, hence
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:35:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:36:\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<1.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:37:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:38:Action \(a=0\) earns zero. Therefore the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every \(T\), despite \(\beta(h)\not\to0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:39:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:40:## Claims Or Lemmas
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:41:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:42:**Lemma 1: Sparse vanishing spikes refute individual-law necessity.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:43:There exists a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\) and a fixed learner with \(R(T)\le 1=o(T)\). This proves the “only if” direction false if interpreted as
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:44:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:45:\exists \pi\; R^\pi_P(T)=o(T) \implies \beta_P(h)\to0
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:46:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:47:for each individual process \(P\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:48:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:49:**Lemma 2: The continuum comparator maximum exists.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:50:For finite \(T\), write
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:51:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:52:M_t=\max_{m:t<s_m\le T} U_m/s_m,
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:53:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:54:with \(M_t=0\) if the set is empty. Then
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:55:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:56:\mathbb E r_t(a)=a\,\Pr[M_t>a].
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:57:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:58:Each \(M_t\) has a continuous distribution on \((0,\infty)\), and the product by \(a\) removes the possible discontinuity at \(0\). The finite sum over \(t\le T\) is continuous on compact \([0,1]\), so the stated \(\max_{a\in[0,1]}\) is attained.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:59:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:60:**Lemma 3: Envelope strengthening.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:61:For any eventually positive nondecreasing envelope \(q(T)=o(T)\), one can build a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\) and an always-zero learner satisfying \(R(T)\le q(T)/2\) for all sufficiently large \(T\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:62:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:63:Take gaps \(s_{n+1}-s_n\to\infty\), for example \(s_n=2^n\), choose \(c_n\in(0,1]\) with \(c_n\le q(s_n)/2\), and set
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:64:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:65:P_{s_n}=c_nU_n/s_n,\qquad P_t=0\text{ otherwise.}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:66:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:67:The same \(a=0\) argument gives \(\beta(h)=1\). If fixed \(a>0\) can trade by horizon \(T\), let \(m^*\) be the last possible crossing spike. Then
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:68:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:69:\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<c_{m^*}\le q(s_{m^*})/2\le q(T)/2.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:70:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:71:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:72:## Proof Attempts
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:73:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:74:The proof audit succeeded. No hidden dependence on rare null events is needed: \(U_n>0\) almost surely, and the conditional probability of a future positive spike is \(1\) for almost every realized history.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:75:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:76:The main quantifier diagnosis is:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:77:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:78:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:79:\beta(h)=\sup_t\sup_a\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{P_{1:t}}
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:80:\Pr[h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{1:t}]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:81:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:82:is too strong for necessity because it is unweighted by payoff \(a\), insensitive to time density, and sensitive to worst-case histories.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:83:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:84:## Gaps And Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:85:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:86:The word “possible” is underdefined. If algorithms may depend on the full process law and horizon, per-process learnability becomes too weak. A meaningful necessity theorem should specify a process class \(\mathcal C\) and ask for one learner with
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:87:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:88:\sup_{P\in\mathcal C} R^\pi_P(T)=o(T).
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:89:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:90:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:91:The counterexample does not rule out a minimax lower bound under stronger assumptions, such as long-delay mass at actions \(a\ge\epsilon\), positive time density, and unpredictability.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:92:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:93:If the action set were \([\epsilon,1]\) instead of \([0,1]\), this specific obstruction disappears.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:94:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:95:## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:96:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:97:The sparse-spike construction is a direct obstruction to unweighted \(\beta\)-necessity.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:98:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:99:The envelope variant shows the obstruction is not just “sublinear”; regret can be made smaller than any prescribed positive nondecreasing \(o(T)\) envelope.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:100:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:101:A similar obstruction likely exists from the essential supremum over histories: rare bad histories can keep \(\beta(h)\) large without contributing linear expected regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:102:
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:103:## Sources Consulted
iterations/iter_001/idea_01/explorer_02.md:104:
