iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:3:The payoff-weighted tail idea is a solid proof route for the upper bound. Define
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:5:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:6:\gamma(h):=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:7:a\,\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid \mathcal F_t).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:8:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:9:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:10:Then the existing truncation proof goes through with \(T\gamma(H)\) replacing \(T\beta(H)\), giving
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:11:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:12:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:13:R(T)\le O(\sqrt{TH\log K})+\frac{T}{K}+T\gamma(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:14:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:15:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:16:Thus \(\gamma(h)\to0\) is sufficient for sublinear regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:17:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:18:I do not think \(\gamma(h)\to0\) is necessary under a process-specific “there exists a no-regret learner for this process” interpretation. There are easy processes with \(\gamma\not\to0\) where the optimal quote is known and fixed, so long delays do not create a learning obstruction.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:19:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:20:## Concrete Progress
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:21:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:22:The truncation comparison strengthens cleanly:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:23:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:24:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:25:0\le r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:26:\le a\,\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:27:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:28:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:29:Taking conditional expectation gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:30:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:31:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:32:\mathbb E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)\mid \mathcal F_t]\le \gamma(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:33:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:34:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:35:Therefore, for any learner,
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:36:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:37:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:38:R(T)\le R^H(T)+T\gamma(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:39:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:40:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:41:Combining with the existing discretization lemma and finite-expert delayed-learning bound gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:42:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:43:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:44:R(T)\le O(\sqrt{TH\log K})+\frac{T}{K}+T\gamma(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:45:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:46:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:47:With \(K=T\) and any \(H(T)\to\infty\) satisfying \(H(T)\log T=o(T)\), this is \(o(T)\) whenever \(\gamma(H)\to0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:48:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:49:## Claims Or Lemmas
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:50:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:51:**Lemma 1: weighted truncation.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:52:For fixed \(H\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:53:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:54:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:55:R(T)\le R^H(T)+T\gamma(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:56:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:57:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:58:Status: proved by the one-line conditional expectation above.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:59:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:60:**Lemma 2: positive-scale equivalence.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:61:Let
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:62:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:63:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:64:\beta_\epsilon(h):=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:65:\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid \mathcal F_t).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:66:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:67:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:68:Then
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:69:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:70:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:71:\gamma(h)\to0
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:72:\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:73:\forall \epsilon>0,\ \beta_\epsilon(h)\to0.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:74:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:75:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:76:Proof: if \(\gamma(h)\to0\), then \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\). Conversely, for any \(\epsilon>0\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:77:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:78:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:79:\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+\beta_\epsilon(h),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:80:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:81:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:82:because actions \(a<\epsilon\) contribute at most \(\epsilon\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:83:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:84:**Lemma 3: average weighted tails are enough for the proof.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:85:A weaker sufficient condition is
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:86:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:87:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:88:\Delta_T(H):=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\sum_{t=1}^T
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:89:\mathbb E\bigl[a\,\mathbf 1\{H<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}\bigr]=o(T)
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:90:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:91:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:92:for some \(H=H(T)\) with \(H\log T=o(T)\). Then the same proof gives
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:93:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:94:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:95:R(T)\le O(\sqrt{TH\log T})+1+\Delta_T(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:96:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:97:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:98:This avoids the essential supremum over rare histories.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:99:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:100:## Proof Attempts
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:101:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:102:The upper-bound proof is straightforward:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:103:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:104:1. Restrict to grid \(\mathcal G_K\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:105:2. Truncate all rewards after \(H\) steps.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:106:3. Since \(P_s\) is public, the truncated reward of every grid action posted at time \(t\) is known by time \(t+H\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:107:4. Apply a standard delayed full-information expert algorithm.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:108:5. Pay \(T/K\) for discretization and \(T\gamma(H)\) for truncation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:109:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:110:The attempted lower-bound proof from \(\gamma\not\to0\) fails. Gamma failure says some positive-payoff quote can have long-delayed trade probability on some history. It does not say the learner needs that delayed feedback to identify a good action.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:111:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:112:## Gaps And Risks
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:113:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:114:The main unresolved issue is quantifiers. If the conjecture means “for every fixed stochastic process, sublinear regret is possible iff the condition holds,” then \(\gamma\to0\) is not necessary. If it means a uniform minimax guarantee over a class of processes, a lower bound may still be possible, but it needs an indistinguishability construction, not just tail failure.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:115:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:116:The current regret definition uses `max` over \([0,1]\). With strict trade condition \(a<P_t\), atoms in \(P_t\) can make the maximum fail to exist. Many arguments are cleaner with `sup`, or with assumptions ensuring existence.
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:117:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:118:## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:119:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:120:Counterexample to necessity under process-specific learnability:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:121:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:122:Choose \(L_m\uparrow\infty\), probabilities \(p_m>0\), and distinct codes \(x_m\in(0,0.1)\). Draw \(M=m\) with probability \(p_m\). Let
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:123:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:124:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:125:P_1=x_M,\qquad
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:126:P_2=\cdots=P_{L_M}=0,\qquad
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:127:P_{L_M+1}=U,
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:128:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:129:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:130:where \(U\sim \mathrm{Unif}[0,1]\), and set later prices to \(0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:131:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:132:For any \(h\), choose \(m\) with \(L_m>h\). Conditional on \(P_1=x_m\), for \(a=1/2\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:133:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:134:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:135:\Pr(h<\tau_1(a)<\infty\mid P_1=x_m)=1/2,
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:136:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:137:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:138:so \(\gamma(h)\ge 1/4\) for all \(h\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:139:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:140:But for any horizon \(T\),
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:141:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:142:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:143:\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)=a(1-a)S_T
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:144:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:145:
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:146:for some nonnegative scalar \(S_T\). The maximizer is always \(a=1/2\). The learner that always posts \(a_t=1/2\) has zero regret for every \(T\), despite \(\gamma\not\to0\).
iterations/iter_001/idea_02/explorer_01.md:147:
