iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s sufficiency bound is basically sound after adding assumptions: exogenous prices, full observation of the price path, `sup` instead of `max`, and a known horizon/truncation scale. It gives a valid nonuniform upper bound.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:5:The characterization angle does not survive. There are simple deterministic binary-spike price paths where both the original `beta` condition and the proposed `Delta_T` condition fail badly, yet regret is O(1).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:7:## Issue List
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:9:- **Missing assumption:** The delayed-grid reduction needs exogenous prices. If future `P_s` can depend on the learner’s quote, counterfactual rewards for unplayed grid arms are not defined from the realized path.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:10:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:11:- **Missing assumption:** The proof is full-information because the learner observes all prices. If the feedback model were only own fills, the `H+1` residue-class Hedge proof would not be implementable.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:12:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:13:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The condition “there exist `H_T,K_T`” gives a nonuniform algorithm unless the learner knows the right truncation schedule. A universal theorem over all processes satisfying the condition needs an adaptive multi-scale argument.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:14:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:15:- **False/overstated:** `Delta_T(H)` is not the exact weakest truncation gap. A sharper comparator-level quantity is
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:16:  \[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:17:  \Gamma_T(H)=\sup_a V_T(a)-\sup_a V_T^H(a),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:18:  \]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:19:  with `Gamma_T(H) <= Delta_T(H)`. `Delta_T` is a clean sufficient bound, not the minimal visible one.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:20:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:21:- **Missing assumption:** The original regret uses `max`, but strict fills `a<P_t` can make the best quote unattained when prices have atoms. Use `sup`, restrict/close the action space differently, or change the fill rule.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:22:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:23:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The rare-history argument separating `beta` from `Delta_T` needs a specified spike-time distribution. A one-spike heavy-tail example can still make `Delta_T` linear unless the truncated first moment is controlled.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:24:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:25:## Counterexamples Or Stress Tests
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:26:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:27:Take a deterministic binary price path:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:28:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:29:P_t=1 \quad\text{iff } t \text{ is a power of two},\qquad P_t=0 \text{ otherwise}.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:30:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:31:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:32:For any `h`, choose a time just after a spike with the next spike more than `h` steps away and take any `a<1`. Then
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:33:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:34:\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{1:t})=1,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:35:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:36:so `beta(h)=1` for all `h`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:37:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:38:For horizons `T=2^m` and any `H=o(T)`,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:39:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:40:\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T),
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:41:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:42:because a constant fraction of quotes before the terminal spike trade only after more than `H` steps.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:43:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:44:Yet the learner that always posts `a_t=1-1/T` has regret at most `1` against the `sup` fixed-action comparator on every binary price sequence: every quote that any `a<1` can get filled also gets filled by `1-1/T`, losing at most `1/T` per filled quote. This is fatal to necessity of both `beta` and `Delta_T` without extra assumptions excluding predictable/endpoint-obvious optima.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:45:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:46:## Literature Or Known-Result Conflicts
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:47:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:48:No direct conflict with delayed-feedback literature. Joulani, György, and Szepesvári’s ICML 2013 delayed-feedback reductions support the finite-delay framing, and Quanrud–Khashabi’s NeurIPS 2015 `O(sqrt(D))` adversarial-delay result is consistent with `D <= T(H+1)`.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:49:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:50:The arm-dependent delay paper by van der Hoeven and Cesa-Bianchi is relevant because quote delays depend on the arm; it may offer sharper best-arm-delay alternatives to uniform truncation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:51:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:52:Recent market-making and bilateral-trade regret papers are adjacent, but their feedback/timing models differ enough that they do not settle this delayed-fill problem.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:53:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:54:## What Survives The Critique
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:55:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:56:A valid proposition should be:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:57:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:58:Under exogenous prices, full price observation, horizon-aware choice of deterministic `H,K`, and `sup` regret,
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:59:\[
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:60:R_T
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:61:\le
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:62:O\!\left(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}\right)
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:63:+\frac{T}{K}
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:64:+\Delta_T(H).
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:65:\]
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:66:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:67:Also, the original uniform conditional tail assumption implies `Delta_T(H) <= T beta(H)`, so `Delta_T` is a genuine sufficiency weakening.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:68:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:69:## Bibliography Candidates
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:70:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:71:- Pooria Joulani, András György, Csaba Szepesvári. “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback.” ICML 2013, PMLR 28(3):1453-1461. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html. Relevant for delayed-feedback reductions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:72:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:73:- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi. “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays.” NeurIPS 2015. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html. Relevant for regret bounds in terms of total delay.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:74:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:75:- Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi. “Nonstochastic Bandits and Experts with Arm-Dependent Delays.” AISTATS 2022, PMLR 151:2022-2044. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v151/van-der-hoeven22a.html. Relevant for quote-dependent delays.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:76:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:77:- Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi, Tommaso Cesari, Roberto Colomboni, Luigi Foscari, Vinayak Pathak. “Market Making without Regret.” COLT 2025, PMLR 291:799-837. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/cesa-bianchi25a.html. Adjacent market-making regret model.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:78:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:79:## Recommended Next Checks
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:80:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:81:Formalize the sufficiency theorem with explicit exogeneity, full-observation, horizon-known, and `sup` assumptions.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:82:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:83:Add the binary-spike counterexample to the counterexample ledger; it breaks the “only if” direction cleanly.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:84:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:85:Decide whether the project wants nonuniform existence results or one universal learner over a tail class.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:86:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:87:Try replacing `Delta_T` by `Gamma_T`, grid-tail quantities, or near-optimal-comparator tail mass.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:88:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_01.md:89:For necessity, add an unpredictability or identification condition, then build a two-environment lower bound where delayed fills are needed to identify the best quote.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:1:## Summary
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:2:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:3:The explorer’s main mathematical take is mostly sound: the horizon-tail quantity `Delta_T(H)` gives a sharper sufficiency condition, but it is not necessary under an instance-level, process-aware interpretation of “sublinear regret is possible.”
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:4:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:5:The largest weakness is quantifier ambiguity. The counterexample does **not** refute a minimax or distribution-free necessity theorem unless the learner is allowed to know the spike schedule and distribution. It does refute necessity for “there exists a learner tailored to this process.”
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:6:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:7:## Issue List
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:8:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:9:1. **Missing assumption: meaning of “possible.”**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:10:   Classification: fatal gap for minimax necessity, not fatal for instance-level necessity.  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:11:   The counterexample uses the learner “always post `1/2`,” which is tailored to the known `Uniform[0,1]` spike law. If the theorem asks for one learner working uniformly over a process class, the counterexample is not enough.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:12:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:13:2. **Plausible but incomplete: sublinear regret proof between spike horizons.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:14:   The explorer’s sentence “same domination argument” needs expansion. A clean repair is: for `S_n <= T < S_{n+1}`, all quotes after `S_n` have zero reward before horizon `T`, so the value is frozen at essentially the `S_n` value. At `T=S_n`, the last block dominates and is optimized by `a=1/2`; all older blocks contribute at most `S_{n-1}=o(S_n)` possible advantage.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:15:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:16:3. **Missing assumption: feedback model for sufficiency.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:17:   Classification: missing assumption.  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:18:   The delayed-grid reduction assumes the learner can reconstruct the full truncated reward vector for all grid quotes after delay `H` from observed prices. This is true if every `P_t` is revealed and the price process is exogenous/counterfactual rewards are well-defined. It is not automatic in a pure bandit/trade-only feedback model.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:19:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:20:4. **Plausible but incomplete: invoking standard delayed expert bounds.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:21:   Classification: plausible but incomplete.  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:22:   The bound `O(sqrt(T H log K))` is reasonable for bounded-delay full-information rewards, but the proof should cite the exact theorem and verify whether it permits adaptive reward sequences, reward revealed before the current action, and horizon-known truncation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:23:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:24:5. **Unsupported if read strongly: literature citations.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:25:   Classification: unsupported citation.  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:26:   The cited delayed-feedback papers support the general reduction style, but none directly proves the proposed `Delta_T(H)` characterization or the non-necessity counterexample.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:27:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:28:## Counterexamples Or Stress Tests
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:29:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:30:- **Spike-process counterexample survives the basic stress test.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:31:  Let `S_n=2^{2^n}`, `P_{S_n}=U_n ~ Uniform[0,1]`, and `P_t=0` otherwise. For `T=S_n`, the last block has length `S_n-S_{n-1}+O(1)` and per-quote expected reward `a(1-a)`, maximized at `a=1/2`. Any advantage from older blocks is bounded by `S_{n-1}=o(T)`. Thus always posting `1/2` has `o(T)` regret.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:32:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:33:- **Linear horizon-tail remains real.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:34:  For any `H_T log T=o(T)`, at `T=S_n`, choosing `a=1/2` gives at least  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:35:  `Delta_T(H_T) >= (1/4)(S_n-S_{n-1}-H_T-O(1)) = Omega(T)`.  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:36:  This supports the explorer’s non-necessity claim under the instance-aware interpretation.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:37:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:38:- **Uniform-learnability stress test.**  
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:39:  If the learner does not know whether spikes are uniform, biased toward high prices, or biased toward low prices, the fixed `1/2` learner may fail. This is exactly where a two-environment lower bound could resurrect a necessity condition.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:40:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:41:## Literature Or Known-Result Conflicts
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:42:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:43:No direct conflict found. The delayed-feedback citations support sufficiency-style reductions, not necessity of raw tail mass.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:44:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:45:- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari give black-box delayed-feedback reductions in ICML 2013, consistent with using bounded-delay expert advice for the truncated problem: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:46:- Quanrud and Khashabi’s NIPS 2015 result controls regret by total delay, also consistent with bounded-delay full-information reasoning: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:47:- Thune, Cesa-Bianchi, and Seldin study unrestricted delays and skipping large delays, thematically close but not the same condition: https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/hash/0e4f5cc9f4f3f7f1651a6b9f9214e5b1-Abstract.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:48:- Cesa-Bianchi, Gentile, and Mansour address composite anonymous feedback, adjacent but a harder/different feedback model: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v75/cesa-bianchi18a.html
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:49:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:50:## What Survives The Critique
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:51:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:52:- The sufficiency lemma with `Delta_T(H)` replacing `T beta(H)` is worth keeping.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:53:- The spike construction is a valid obstruction to instance-level necessity.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:54:- The key conceptual takeaway survives: raw delayed reward mass is not the right necessary object; the missing ingredient is informational unpredictability of the tail.
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:55:
iterations/iter_001/idea_03/critic_02_on_explorer_02.md:56:## Bibliography Candidates
