working_notes/canonical_summary.md:1:# Canonical Summary
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:2:
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:3:We study delayed market making where each public price \(P_t\in[0,1]\) is revealed, the learner posts \(a_t\in[0,1]\), and a quote \(a\) posted at \(t\) pays once, \(r_t(a)=a\,1\{\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}\), if some future price strictly exceeds it. Regret should usually be written against \(\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\), not \(\max\), unless comparator attainment is separately proved; strict crossing and atoms can destroy maxima.
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:4:
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:5:Iteration 1 refutes the stated "only if" direction under the literal instance-wise/process-aware reading. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Thus unweighted uniform tail decay is sufficient-looking but not necessary for instance-wise sublinear regret.
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:6:
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:7:The sufficiency side survives under explicit assumptions: public exogenous prices define counterfactual truncated rewards for all grid quotes, the learner uses full-information delayed-expert feedback, \(H,K\) are known/chosen, and regret uses a supremum comparator. Then
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:8:\[
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:9:R_T \lesssim \sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}+T/K+T\beta(H).
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:10:\]
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:11:This improves to \(T\gamma(H)\) for the payoff-weighted tail
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:12:\[
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:13:\gamma(h)=\sup_{t,a}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t} a\,\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid\mathcal F_t),
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:14:\]
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:15:and further to a horizon-tail loss
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:16:\[
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:17:\Delta_T(H)=\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}\mathbb E[a\,1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}].
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:18:\]
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:19:However neither \(\gamma(h)\to0\) nor \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\) is established as necessary; reports give counterexamples where long positive-payoff tails are sparse, predictable, or process-known.
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:20:
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:21:The main unresolved issue is quantifiers. A true necessity theorem must specify whether "possible" means instance-wise existence of a process-aware learner, a universal learner over a process class, or a minimax/distribution-free guarantee. The promising necessity route is no longer raw delay-tail mass, but an information-theoretic condition: unresolved delayed payoff mass must be unpredictable and necessary to identify the best fixed quote.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:1:# Lemma Bank
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:2:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:3:## LB-001: Downward Grid Discretization
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:4:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:5:For \(\mathcal G_K=\{0,1/K,\ldots,(K-1)/K\}\), round any \(a\in[0,1]\) down to \(b(a)\in\mathcal G_K\). Since \(b\le a\), every future price exceeding \(a\) also exceeds \(b\), and
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:6:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:7:r_t^H(a)\le r_t^H(b)+1/K.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:8:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:9:Therefore \(R_T^H\le R_{T,K}^H+T/K\). This is accepted.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:10:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:11:## LB-002: Bounded-Delay Full-Information Experts By Residues
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:12:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:13:If each reward vector in \([0,1]^K\) is revealed after at most \(H\) rounds, split time into \(H+1\) residue classes and run independent full-information Hedge instances. On each class, previous rewards have matured before the next action in the class. Summing the per-class regrets and applying Cauchy-Schwarz gives
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:14:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:15:R_{T,K}^H=O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}).
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:16:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:17:Status: accepted as standard/provable, but final writeup should state adversary/adaptivity conventions.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:18:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:19:## LB-003: Weighted Truncation
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:20:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:21:For fixed \(H\),
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:22:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:23:0\le r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)=a\,1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:24:\le a\,1\{H<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:25:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:26:Taking conditional expectations and using \(\gamma(H)\) gives
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:27:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:28:R_T\le R_T^H+T\gamma(H).
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:29:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:30:Status: accepted.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:31:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:32:## LB-004: Horizon-Tail Truncation
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:33:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:34:Let
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:35:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:36:\Delta_T(H)=\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}\mathbb E[a\,1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}].
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:37:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:38:For every fixed comparator \(a\), truncation loses exactly the summand above, while the learner's true reward pathwise dominates its truncated reward. Hence
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:39:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:40:R_T\le R_T^H+\Delta_T(H).
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:41:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:42:Combining with LB-001 and LB-002 gives the \(\Delta_T\)-sufficiency bound. Status: accepted.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:43:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:44:## LB-005: Positive-Scale Equivalence
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:45:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:46:For
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:47:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:48:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:49:\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid\mathcal F_t),
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:50:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:51:\[
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:52:\gamma(h)\to0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:53:\forall\epsilon>0,\ \beta_\epsilon(h)\to0.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:54:\]
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:55:Status: accepted.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:56:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:57:## LB-006: Sparse Vanishing-Spike Counterexample
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:58:
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:59:Let \(s_n=2^n\), \(P_{s_n}=U_n/s_n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise. Then at \(t=s_n\), \(a=0\) has \(\tau_{s_n}(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n=2^n\) almost surely, so \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\). For fixed \(a>0\), if \(m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}\) exists, only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can earn and \(\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<1\); if it does not exist, reward is zero. Thus \(R_T\le1\) for the always-zero learner. Status: accepted.
