working_notes/claim_ledger.md:3:## Accepted Or Strongly Supported
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:4:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:5:**CL-001: Truncated-grid sufficiency under full-information assumptions.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:6:Status: accepted modulo standard delayed-expert theorem and explicit model assumptions.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:7:Claim: If public exogenous prices allow the learner to compute every grid arm's truncated reward \(r_t^H(b)\) by time \(t+H\), then a \(K\)-grid delayed full-information learner satisfies
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:8:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:9:R_T \le O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K})+T/K+T\beta(H).
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:10:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:11:Dependencies: public full price observation; exogenous or realized-path counterfactual rewards; bounded rewards; deterministic known \(H,K\); comparator written as \(\sup\) unless attainment is proved.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:12:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:13:**CL-002: Payoff-weighted tail suffices.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:14:Status: accepted.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:15:Claim: With
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:16:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:17:\gamma(h)=\sup_t\sup_a\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:18:a\,\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid\mathcal F_t),
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:19:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:20:the same proof gives
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:21:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:22:R_T \le O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K})+T/K+T\gamma(H).
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:23:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:24:Thus \(\gamma(h)\to0\) is a sufficient condition for sublinear regret after choosing \(K=T\) and \(H(T)\to\infty\) with \(H(T)\log T=o(T)\).
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:25:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:26:**CL-003: Positive-scale equivalence for \(\gamma\).**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:27:Status: accepted.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:28:Let
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:29:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:30:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:31:\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid\mathcal F_t).
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:32:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:33:Then \(\gamma(h)\to0\) iff \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\to0\) for every \(\epsilon>0\). Proof: \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\), while \(\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+\beta_\epsilon(h)\).
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:34:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:35:**CL-004: Horizon-tail sufficiency.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:36:Status: accepted as a sufficient nonuniform condition.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:37:Define
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:38:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:39:\Delta_T(H)=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\sum_{t\le T}\mathbb E[a\,1\{H<\tau_t(a)\le T-t\}].
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:40:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:41:Then
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:42:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:43:R_T \le O(\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K})+T/K+\Delta_T(H).
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:44:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:45:If known deterministic schedules satisfy \(H_T\log K_T=o(T)\), \(T/K_T=o(T)\), and \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\), the delayed-grid algorithm has sublinear regret. A universal theorem over unknown schedules still needs model selection or skipping.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:46:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:47:**CL-005: Sparse vanishing spikes refute instance-wise \(\beta\)-necessity.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:48:Status: accepted.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:49:For \(s_n=2^n\), \(P_{s_n}=U_n/s_n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, with iid \(U_n\sim{\rm Unif}(0,1)\), one has \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but \(\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)\le1\). The always-zero learner therefore has \(R_T\le1\). This refutes
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:50:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:51:\exists \pi:\ R_P^\pi(T)=o(T)\Rightarrow \beta_P(h)\to0
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:52:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:53:for individual processes.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:54:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:55:## Refuted Or Weakened
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:56:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:57:**CL-006: Original iff conjecture.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:58:Status: refuted under literal instance-wise reading; unresolved under class-uniform/minimax readings.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:59:Reason: CL-005.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:60:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:61:**CL-007: \(\gamma(h)\to0\) as an instance-wise necessary condition.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:62:Status: refuted for process-aware/instance-wise learnability.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:63:Reason: coded-delay construction can have \(\gamma(h)\ge1/4\) for all \(h\), yet the process makes \(a=1/2\) always optimal and an always-\(1/2\) learner has zero regret.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:64:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:65:**CL-008: \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\) as an instance-wise necessary condition.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:66:Status: refuted or at least strongly weakened.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:67:Reason: predictable sparse spike examples have linear horizon-tail loss along infinitely many horizons while a horizon/process-aware learner can quote the known near-optimal level and get \(o(T)\) regret.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:68:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:69:**CL-009: \(\Delta_T\) is the weakest visible truncation gap.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:70:Status: false as stated.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:71:A smaller comparator-level quantity is
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:72:\[
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:73:\Gamma_T(H)=\sup_a V_T(a)-\sup_a V_T^H(a),
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:74:\]
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:75:with \(\Gamma_T(H)\le\Delta_T(H)\). Even \(\Gamma_T\)-type raw reward tails may not be necessary if delayed mass is predictable.
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:76:
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:77:**CL-010: Comparator maxima always exist.**  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:78:Status: false.  
working_notes/claim_ledger.md:79:With strict crossing \(P_s>a\), atoms can make the best fixed quote unattained; e.g. deterministic future price \(c\) gives payoff approaching \(c\) as \(a\uparrow c\) but not at \(a=c\). Use \(\sup\) or add assumptions ensuring attainment.
