working_notes/counterexamples.md:1:# Counterexamples And Obstructions
working_notes/counterexamples.md:2:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:3:## CE-001: Sparse Vanishing Spikes Refute \(\beta\)-Necessity
working_notes/counterexamples.md:4:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:5:Status: accepted.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:6:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:7:Let \(s_n=2^n\), \(U_n\stackrel{iid}{\sim}{\rm Unif}(0,1)\), and
working_notes/counterexamples.md:8:\[
working_notes/counterexamples.md:9:P_t=\begin{cases}
working_notes/counterexamples.md:10:U_n/s_n,&t=s_n,\\
working_notes/counterexamples.md:11:0,&\text{otherwise}.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:12:\end{cases}
working_notes/counterexamples.md:13:\]
working_notes/counterexamples.md:14:For \(a=0\) at \(t=s_n\), the next positive price occurs at \(s_{n+1}\) almost surely, so \(\tau_{s_n}(0)=2^n\). Therefore \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\). Yet for every horizon \(T\), every fixed \(a>0\) has expected reward \(<1\) by comparing against the last possible crossing spike, and \(a=0\) has zero reward. The always-zero learner has \(R_T\le1\). This refutes the stated only-if direction for individual processes.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:15:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:16:Notes: This uses \(0\in[0,1]\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), the specific witness disappears once all spike heights are below \(\epsilon\). If \(0\) is excluded but arbitrarily small actions remain, a related supremum pathology remains but needs a separate admissible learner argument.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:17:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:18:## CE-002: Comparator Maximum Can Fail
working_notes/counterexamples.md:19:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:20:Status: accepted.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:21:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:22:If a future public price is deterministically \(c\in(0,1]\), then quotes \(a<c\) trade while \(a\ge c\) do not. The fixed-action payoff approaches the value at \(c\) as \(a\uparrow c\) but is not attained at \(a=c\). Thus the problem should use \(\sup_a\) or add conditions ensuring a maximum exists.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:23:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:24:## CE-003: Coded Delay Refutes Instance-Wise \(\gamma\)-Necessity
working_notes/counterexamples.md:25:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:26:Status: accepted as a stress-test construction; writeup still needs final polishing.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:27:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:28:Draw \(M=m\) with \(p_m>0\), reveal a code \(P_1=x_m\), set \(P_2=\cdots=P_{L_m}=0\), set \(P_{L_m+1}=U\sim{\rm Unif}[0,1]\), and set later prices to zero, with \(L_m\to\infty\). For any \(h\), choose \(m\) with \(L_m>h\). Conditional on \(P_1=x_m\), for \(a=1/2\),
working_notes/counterexamples.md:29:\[
working_notes/counterexamples.md:30:\Pr(h<\tau_1(a)<\infty\mid P_1=x_m)=1/2,
working_notes/counterexamples.md:31:\]
working_notes/counterexamples.md:32:so \(\gamma(h)\ge1/4\). But for each horizon the expected fixed-action value is \(a(1-a)S_T\) for some scalar \(S_T\ge0\), so \(a=1/2\) is optimal and the always-\(1/2\) learner has zero regret.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:33:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:34:## CE-004: Sparse Blocks Break Uniform-In-Time Tail Necessity
working_notes/counterexamples.md:35:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:36:Status: plausible; needs formal all-prefix proof if used.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:37:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:38:Consider deterministic epochs \(0^n1^{n^3}\). For any \(h\), choose \(n>h\) and a quote \(a=1/2\) at the first zero of that block; the delay to a one exceeds \(h\), so \(\gamma(h)\ge1/2\). But through epoch \(N\), zero-block positions are \(O(N^2)\) while total time is \(O(N^4)\), so ex-ante averaged long-tail mass is plausibly \(o(T)\). This shows \(\sup_t\) conditions can be too strong for initial expected regret.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:39:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:40:## CE-005: Predictable Sparse Spikes Refute \(\Delta_T\)-Necessity
working_notes/counterexamples.md:41:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:42:Status: accepted as obstruction, with exact variant to formalize.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:43:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:44:Binary power-of-two variant: \(P_t=1\) iff \(t\) is a power of two, otherwise \(P_t=0\). At horizons \(T=2^m\), any \(H=o(T)\) leaves \(\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T)\) because a constant fraction of quotes before the terminal spike trade only after more than \(H\). Yet a horizon-aware learner posting close to \(1\), e.g. \(1-1/T\), gets within \(O(1)\) of the fixed-action supremum on binary paths.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:45:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:46:Uniform superexponential variant: let \(S_n=2^{2^n}\), \(P_{S_n}=U_n\sim{\rm Unif}[0,1]\), and \(P_t=0\) otherwise. The always-\(1/2\) learner is optimal on the dominant resolved block at \(T=S_n\), while older blocks have length \(S_{n-1}=o(S_n)\). However \(\Delta_{S_n}(H_T)\ge (S_n-S_{n-1}-H_T)/4=\Omega(S_n)\) for any \(H_T=o(T)\). Need a clean all-horizons regret bound for \(S_n\le T<S_{n+1}\).
working_notes/counterexamples.md:47:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:48:## CE-006: Rare-History Essential-Sup Obstruction
working_notes/counterexamples.md:49:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:50:Status: conjectural stress test.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:51:
working_notes/counterexamples.md:52:A tiny-probability branch containing arbitrarily long positive-payoff delays can keep an essential-sup tail condition large while contributing negligible ex-ante expected regret. A concrete one-spike heavy-tail construction was suggested but not fully verified; future work should compute both the residual conditional tail and \(\Delta_T(H)\).
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:1:# Iteration Summaries
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:2:
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:3:## Iteration 1
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:4:
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:5:Main outcome: the stated iff is false under the literal instance-wise interpretation. The sparse vanishing-spike process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but the always-zero learner has \(R_T\le1\).
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:6:
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:7:Accepted progress: the "if" proof can be made rigorous with explicit exogenous/public-price and full-information delayed-grid assumptions. The truncation term can be \(T\beta(H)\), \(T\gamma(H)\), or the sharper horizon-tail \(\Delta_T(H)\). The positive-scale equivalence for \(\gamma\) is sound.
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:8:
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:9:Refuted/weakened: \(\beta\)-necessity, \(\gamma\)-necessity, and \(\Delta_T\)-necessity all fail or are unproved for process-aware instance-level learnability. Comparator `max` is also unsafe under strict crossing; use `sup`.
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:10:
working_notes/iteration_summaries.md:11:Next focus: formalize the sufficient theorem and counterexamples, then choose the intended quantifiers. If pursuing necessity, switch to a two-environment indistinguishability lower bound measuring unpredictable, decision-relevant tail mass.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:1:# Promising Directions
working_notes/promising_directions.md:2:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:3:## 1. Formalize The Clean Sufficient Theorem
working_notes/promising_directions.md:4:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:5:Write a theorem with \(\sup\)-regret, exogenous/public prices, full-information truncated grid rewards, integer deterministic \(H,K\), and one of the tail penalties:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:6:\[
working_notes/promising_directions.md:7:T\beta(H),\qquad T\gamma(H),\qquad \Delta_T(H).
working_notes/promising_directions.md:8:\]
working_notes/promising_directions.md:9:Include the residue-class Hedge lemma or cite a precise delayed full-information theorem. This is the safest accepted progress to turn into a polished result.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:10:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:11:## 2. Fix The Quantifiers Before Any Necessity Work
working_notes/promising_directions.md:12:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:13:Create a taxonomy:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:14:- instance-wise/process-aware learner;
working_notes/promising_directions.md:15:- process-known but horizon-oblivious learner;
working_notes/promising_directions.md:16:- one universal learner over a specified class;
working_notes/promising_directions.md:17:- minimax lower bound over processes failing a condition;
working_notes/promising_directions.md:18:- uniform post-history guarantee.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:19:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:20:The original only-if statement is false for the first interpretation. A meaningful necessity theorem likely needs one of the latter interpretations.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:21:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:22:## 3. Promote And Polish Counterexamples
working_notes/promising_directions.md:23:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:24:Formalize CE-001 fully as a proposition. Then write CE-003 and CE-005 with exact horizon quantifiers. In particular, finish the all-horizons proof for the superexponential uniform-spike example and decide whether the binary power-of-two example requires horizon-aware actions.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:25:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:26:## 4. Replace Raw Tail Necessity By An Information Condition
working_notes/promising_directions.md:27:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:28:Attempt a two-environment lower bound. Construct two price laws/classes with indistinguishable public histories before delayed fills resolve, but different optimal fixed quotes. The lower bound should use payoff-relevant delayed mass that is both frequent and unpredictable.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:29:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:30:## 5. Adaptive Truncation And Skipping Algorithms
working_notes/promising_directions.md:31:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:32:Translate arbitrary-delay/skipping literature to the grid market-making setting. Goal: avoid a known \(H_T\) by running multi-scale truncations or skip-set algorithms, and bound regret by skipped payoff mass or counted total delay rather than a hard uniform horizon.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:33:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:34:## 6. Threshold Geometry And Discretization
working_notes/promising_directions.md:35:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:36:Exploit \(r_t(a)=a\,1\{\max_{s>t}P_s>a\}\). Prices reveal nested threshold information across quotes. Possible payoffs: improved discretization, adaptive grids, or one-sided Lipschitz methods. Keep this separate from delay-tail characterization until the basic theorem is settled.
working_notes/promising_directions.md:37:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:38:## 7. Rare-History Stress Tests
working_notes/promising_directions.md:39:
working_notes/promising_directions.md:40:Build a fully specified rare-branch process where \(\operatorname{ess\,sup}\) tails fail but expected regret and \(\Delta_T\) remain small. This will test whether \(\gamma\) is too strong even for positive payoff scale under initial expected regret.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:1:# Failed Attempts
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:2:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:3:## FA-001: Proving Necessity From Unweighted \(\beta\)
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:4:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:5:Failed. The sparse vanishing-spike process has \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\) because \(a=0\) waits across arbitrarily long gaps, yet all fixed comparator value is \(O(1)\). Long finite hitting times at zero or vanishing payoff scale do not imply regret hardness.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:6:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:7:## FA-002: Treating \(\gamma(h)\to0\) As Necessary
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:8:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:9:Failed for instance-wise/process-aware learnability. A coded-delay process can reveal which long delay will occur, keep \(\gamma(h)\ge1/4\), and still make \(a=1/2\) the known optimal quote for every horizon. Positive-payoff delayed tails alone are not a learning obstruction.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:10:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:11:## FA-003: Treating Horizon-Tail Loss \(\Delta_T\) As A Characterization
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:12:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:13:Failed or strongly weakened. Predictable sparse spikes can make \(\Delta_T(H_T)=\Omega(T)\) along infinitely many horizons for every \(H_T\log T=o(T)\), while a process-aware learner posts a known near-optimal quote and gets sublinear regret. Raw delayed reward mass is not the same as information needed for learning.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:14:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:15:## FA-004: Using Comparator `max` Without Checking Attainment
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:16:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:17:Failed. Strict trade condition \(P_s>a\) and atomic prices can make the best fixed action unattained. Future price constantly equal to \(c\) gives a payoff supremum as \(a\uparrow c\), but action \(a=c\) does not trade. Future writeups should use \(\sup\) unless continuity/no-atom conditions are proved.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:18:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:19:## FA-005: Citing Delayed-Feedback Literature Without The Model Map
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:20:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:21:Incomplete. Delayed-learning results support the finite-grid step only after stating that prices are public/exogenous and that all truncated grid rewards are computable after at most \(H\) rounds. Bandit, anonymous, and composite-delay references are often adjacent rather than direct support for this full-information reduction.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:22:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:23:## FA-006: Rare-History Obstruction As A Lemma
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:24:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:25:Not yet proved. Reports repeatedly suggest that rare positive-probability histories can keep an essential supremum large while contributing negligible expected regret, but no complete process and horizon calculation has been written. Keep as a candidate stress test.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:26:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:27:## FA-007: Overstrong Envelope Claim
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:28:
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:29:The claim "regret bounded by any prescribed sublinear envelope" was overbroad if the envelope is not eventually positive/nondecreasing or if it tends to zero. A corrected version should be stated only for sufficiently large horizons and with explicit spike amplitudes \(c_n>0\), \(c_n/s_n\le1\), and \(c_n\le q(s_n)/4\) after the finite prefix.

