working_notes/literature_map.md:1:# Literature Map
working_notes/literature_map.md:2:
working_notes/literature_map.md:3:## Full-Information Delayed Online Learning
working_notes/literature_map.md:4:
working_notes/literature_map.md:5:- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (ICML/PMLR 2013) is the primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction. Verified source states the ICML 2013/PMLR venue and frames delay effects on regret; use this for the finite-grid delayed-learning step, after explicitly mapping public price observations to delayed full-information reward vectors.
working_notes/literature_map.md:6:- Quanrud and Khashabi (NeurIPS/NIPS 2015) gives adversarial-delay regret in terms of total delay \(D\), with source page reporting \(O(\sqrt D)\)-type control. This supports bounded-delay scaling via \(D\lesssim TH\), but still needs model mapping.
working_notes/literature_map.md:7:- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference. It was reported by agents but not source-verified in this synthesis; keep as background candidate.
working_notes/literature_map.md:8:- Van der Hoeven and Cesa-Bianchi (AISTATS/PMLR 2022) treats arm-dependent delays in nonstochastic bandits and experts. Verified source says the full-information experts setting has first-order bounds depending on delays/losses of the best arm. Relevant because quote delays are arm-dependent.
working_notes/literature_map.md:9:
working_notes/literature_map.md:10:## Unrestricted, Skipping, And Aggregate Delay Bounds
working_notes/literature_map.md:11:
working_notes/literature_map.md:12:- Thune, Cesa-Bianchi, and Seldin (NeurIPS 2019) handles nonstochastic bandits with unbounded delays and skipping excessively delayed observations; verified source gives bounds involving total delay and skip/oracle quantities. Useful for replacing a hard \(H\) truncation by adaptive skipping.
working_notes/literature_map.md:13:- Zimmert and Seldin (AISTATS 2020/arXiv:1910.06054) gives optimal arbitrary-delay adversarial bandit bounds with refined excluded-round sets. Verified through arXiv; relevance is conceptual for skip-set tail penalties.
working_notes/literature_map.md:14:- Lancewicki, Segal, Koren, and Mansour (ICML/PMLR 2021) studies stochastic bandits with unrestricted and infinite delays, with quantile-based delay dependence. Verified source; relevant for quantile alternatives to uniform tails.
working_notes/literature_map.md:15:- Zierahn, van der Hoeven, Lancewicki, Rosenberg, and Cesa-Bianchi (JMLR 2025) gives a modern unified analysis for delayed nonstochastic bandit feedback. Verified source; useful terminology but less direct because the truncated market-making reduction is full-information.
working_notes/literature_map.md:16:- Bistritz, Zhou, Chen, Bambos, and Blanchet (NeurIPS 2019) studies delayed adversarial bandit feedback and missing feedback beyond horizon. Verified source; useful for horizon-censoring distinctions, not direct proof support.
working_notes/literature_map.md:17:- Gyorgy and Joulani (ICML 2021 candidate from idea JSON) and Flaspohler et al. (ICML 2021 candidate) remain unchecked candidates for adaptive/optimistic delayed online learning.
working_notes/literature_map.md:18:
working_notes/literature_map.md:19:## Market Making, Pricing, And Threshold Geometry
working_notes/literature_map.md:20:
working_notes/literature_map.md:21:- Cesa-Bianchi, Cesari, Colomboni, Foscari, and Pathak, "Market Making without Regret" (COLT/PMLR 2025), verified. Adjacent market-making regret model; useful for terminology and relation to pricing/auction structure, but not the same delayed-fill model.
working_notes/literature_map.md:22:- Abernethy and Kale, "Adaptive Market Making via Online Learning" (NeurIPS 2013), verified from the NeurIPS PDF. It assumes exogenous price series and online learning over market-making strategies; relevant background for exogenous-price modeling.
working_notes/literature_map.md:23:- Dutting, Guruganesh, Schneider, and Wang (ICML/PMLR 2023 candidate) on one-sided Lipschitz functions remains unchecked. Potentially relevant for threshold reward geometry and improved discretization.
working_notes/literature_map.md:24:
working_notes/literature_map.md:25:## Composite Or Anonymous Feedback
working_notes/literature_map.md:26:
working_notes/literature_map.md:27:- Cesa-Bianchi, Gentile, and Mansour (COLT/PMLR 2018), verified, studies composite anonymous feedback where losses are spread over bounded windows and only aggregates are observed. This is harder/different than the public-price model, so use only as comparison.
working_notes/literature_map.md:28:- Pike-Burke, Agrawal, Szepesvari, and Grunewalder (ICML 2018/arXiv candidate) on delayed aggregated anonymous feedback remains unchecked; likely background rather than direct support.
working_notes/literature_map.md:29:
working_notes/literature_map.md:30:## Current Takeaway
working_notes/literature_map.md:31:
working_notes/literature_map.md:32:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, or skipped delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, or horizon-censored delay quantities, but the iteration's counterexamples show even raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability or information acquisition.

